// THE IMAGE THAT DOES NOT EXIST YET, BUILT AS A MATERIALIZATION RATHER THAN RUN AS A SCRIPT.
//
// WHAT THIS LANE IS FOR. gunbc.spark.serving_runtime_capability_probe measured every published GB10
// container image against the five capabilities DeepSeek V4.1 needs and found none that registers
// DeepseekV41ForCausalLM on sm_121. Its conclusion was not "no image works" but a BUILD OBLIGATION:
// the only source known to contain the V4.1 path is vLLM at 07b75534651a0dd584c6f3be75d8c62fb8426397,
// and the way to settle what that source yields on compute capability 12.1 is to build it and ask the
// same questions inside the result. This module is that build.
//
// WHY IT IS NOT A SHELL SCRIPT SOMEBODY RAN. A `docker build` invocation typed into a terminal
// produces an image whose provenance is a memory: which base, which arch list, which revision. The
// DESIGN §6 tell for that is out-of-band actuation, and its cost here is concrete rather than
// stylistic -- the whole product of this lane is a DIGEST whose inputs someone else must be able to
// re-derive, and an image whose arch list nobody recorded is an image nobody can rebuild or refute.
// So the inputs are declarations, the identity of the build is a content hash OVER those
// declarations, and the invocation is derived from them through the same ArgvCommand wall every
// other tool invocation in this repository passes.
//
// THE PRINCIPAL IS THE MANAGED EXECUTOR, NOT THE OPERATOR, AND THAT WAS A SECOND LITERAL IN THE SAME
// PLACE AS THE FIRST. An earlier revision authenticated as gunbc.spark.managed_access_bootstrap's
// OPERATOR bootstrap login -- the human account, which exists to take a PASSWORD on first contact and
// refuses key authentication by design on every Spark. It worked only on the hosts where a human had
// already installed a key by hand, which is precisely the provisioning state this lane should not be
// depending on. `spark_managed_executor_login` is the account the modeled bootstrap route converges,
// and the one the fleet key is laid for; naming it here is naming the authority rather than the
// account that happened to work.
//
// TWO TRANSPORTS, AND WHICH ONE A LEG TAKES IS DECIDED BY WHAT THE LEG NEEDS.
//
// The GIT legs run through gunbc.typed_argv_exec typed_argv_exec_over_fleet_ssh: a
// portable-word-sealed argv over an SSH leg whose known-hosts anchor was materialized and read back
// and whose credential was verified against the declared fleet key before any host was contacted.
// They need no root, and the managed executor can perform them.
//
// THE DOCKER LEGS CANNOT GO THAT WAY, AND AN EARLIER VERSION OF THIS PARAGRAPH CLAIMED THE OPPOSITE AS
// A MEASURED FACT. It read that no sudo appears in this module and that the build reaches docker
// through the operator's docker group membership. Both halves were wrong together: the legs
// authenticate as the MANAGED EXECUTOR, not the operator, and that account is in no docker group on
// any Spark -- which this lane's own sibling module records as its measured finding. The claim
// survived because the one build that ever ran took the socket on srv5, a host carrying a
// hand-installed key for the human briansrls AND that account in the docker group: provisioning drift
// no modeled route produces, standing in for a mechanism nobody had. The docker legs now bind to
// gunbc.spark.model_snapshot_materialize spark_remote_run, the single route the corpus models for
// that daemon, which is privileged by necessity rather than by preference.

// ── CONFORMANCE, MATERIALIZATION DOMAIN (DESIGN §3b): WHAT THIS INHABITS AND WHERE IT DIVERGES ──
//
// INHABITED. The identity of a build is minted by std.artifact_store artifact_realization_digest over
// a list of NAMED realization inputs -- the authority that already answers "a set of named inputs
// folded into one content identity". The tag is derived from that key and from nothing else.
//
// DIVERGES, WITH THE REASON. std.materialization_provider's ArtifactRequest is a CLOSED coproduct
// over three compiler requests (parse a module, typecheck a module, resolve a closure), each keyed by
// a source or closure digest and a DeclaredInput compiler. A container image built by a foreign
// toolchain on another machine is none of those, and widening that coproduct to admit it would make
// the compiler's own provider vocabulary carry a fleet concern -- the layer inversion §3 names, not a
// missing row. std.realization's Materialization (Recompute | Memoize | Share) likewise decides how a
// step INSIDE one program's demand graph is realized; it has no place to say which host ran a build or
// what digest came back. So this module consumes the keying authority and does NOT route through the
// provider or step-realization ones, deliberately.
//
// WHAT WOULD DISSOLVE THE DIVERGENCE. Not a new ArtifactRequest arm: an artifact-store-shaped surface
// whose subject is an EXTERNAL build -- inputs, a host, a produced digest -- which does not exist in
// std today and which one lane's container build is not enough evidence to design. The second
// external-build consumer is the trigger; until then this is a stated departure rather than a fork,
// because nothing here re-answers a question those two modules already answer.

// ── WHERE A BUILD MAY RUN ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// NO HOST IS NAMED IN THIS MODULE, AND THAT IS THE POINT. An earlier revision carried a literal
// build host, and the host it carried was a member of the Spark serving pair -- policy standing in a
// literal, which is the §3 tell exactly: "where may this run" is a workflow decision, not a property
// of a vLLM build. The entry takes its host from the caller and admits it against a DERIVED
// admissibility fact, so the roster cannot go stale against the fleet it describes.
//
// ADMISSIBILITY IS A SUBTRACTION FROM THE MODELED FLEET, not a written list. Every Spark carrying a
// router binding is a candidate; a member of a group that is ACTUALLY SERVING is excluded, because
// that is where an engine runs; a member holding an undischarged llama.cpp RPC peer reservation is
// excluded, because that reservation is someone else's. Adding a Spark, or standing a second group up
// into spark_pair_serving_groups, changes this answer with no edit here.
//
// IT IS THE ACTIVE GROUPS, NOT EVERY FABRIC GROUP, AND THE FIRST CUT GOT THAT WRONG. A FabricGroup is
// a 400G cage -- four hosts sharing one breakout -- so subtracting every group subtracted the entire
// fleet and left nothing admissible, which the witness caught. A cage is where a group COULD serve;
// gunbc.spark.pair_serving_desired spark_pair_serving_groups is where one DOES. Those are different
// facts and only the second is a reservation.
fn spark_build_host_excluded(host: HostIdentity) -> Bool {
  let serving = flat_map(spark_pair_serving_groups, g => fabric_group_hosts(g: g))
  any(serving, h => host_identity_eq(a: h, b: host))
    || llama_cpp_rpc_host_holds_undischarged_reservation(host: host)
}

fn admissible_spark_build_hosts() -> List<HostIdentity> {
  filter(dgx_spark_reserved_identities(), h => !spark_build_host_excluded(host: h))
}

fn spark_build_host_admissible(host: HostIdentity) -> Bool {
  any(admissible_spark_build_hosts(), h => host_identity_eq(a: h, b: host))
}

// A HOST THE CALLER NAMED IS NOT YET A HOST THIS LANE MAY USE. The refusal is separated from the
// endpoint lookup because they fail for different reasons and want different remedies: an
// inadmissible host is a policy answer, an unresolvable one is a fleet-data answer, and an earlier
// revision of this lane learned the second the hard way.
type SparkBuildHostAdmission
  = SparkBuildHostAdmitted { host: HostIdentity }
  | SparkBuildHostRefused { cause: String }

fn admit_spark_build_host(raw: String) -> SparkBuildHostAdmission {
  let host = raw as HostIdentity
  if raw == "" {
    SparkBuildHostRefused { cause: "no build host was named; this entry takes one and does not pick" }
  } else if !spark_build_host_admissible(host: host) {
    SparkBuildHostRefused {
      cause: join([
        raw, " is not an admissible build host. Admissible today: ",
        join(map(admissible_spark_build_hosts(), h => h as String), ", "),
        ". A Spark is excluded when it is a member of a fabric serving group or holds an undischarged llama.cpp RPC peer reservation",
      ], ""),
    }
  } else {
    SparkBuildHostAdmitted { host: host }
  }
}

// THE BUILD ROOT IS DERIVED FROM THE PRINCIPAL THAT OWNS IT, NOT SPELLED. An earlier revision put it
// under the OPERATOR's home while the legs authenticate as the managed executor -- a path belonging to
// one account written into work performed by another, which is a permission error waiting at the far
// side and reads as a broken build rather than as the wrong account being named.
// gunbc.spark.managed_access_bootstrap already derives a principal's home for exactly this reason.
data vllm_build_root_name: NonEmptyStr = "gunbc-vllm-build" as NonEmptyStr

fn vllm_build_root() -> NonEmptyStr {
  join([spark_managed_principal_home(login: spark_managed_executor_login as NonEmptyStr) as String, "/", vllm_build_root_name as String], "") as NonEmptyStr
}

// ── ONE WORKSPACE PER REQUEST, BECAUSE ONE SHARED TREE MAKES A SECOND RECIPE UNSERVEABLE ──────
//
// A SINGLE `/src` WAS CORRECT WHILE EXACTLY ONE RECIPE EXISTED and becomes a refusal the moment a
// second one does. The converger rightly will not move an already-populated checkout to another
// revision -- a build may be reading it -- so with one directory the second recipe can only ever be
// told that the tree belongs to the first. That is the right answer to the wrong question: the two
// builds do not want the same tree.
//
// THE DIRECTORY IS KEYED BY THE REVISION, which is the identity that decides what may be checked out
// there. Two recipes at one revision legitimately share a tree; two revisions never do. Keying on the
// full build request instead would re-clone for a parallelism change that cannot affect a checkout.
//
// THE ALTERNATIVE WAS A FORCE-CHECKOUT and it is worse than it looks: `git checkout -f` in a tree
// another build is compiling from does not fail, it silently changes the sources underneath it, and
// the resulting image records a provenance that was true when the build started.
fn vllm_source_dir_for(source: VllmSourceRevision) -> NonEmptyStr {
  join([vllm_build_root() as String, "/src/", source.full_revision as String], "") as NonEmptyStr
}

// THE LEGACY LOCATION IS KEPT FOR THE ROW THAT ALREADY OCCUPIES IT. The V4.1 build materialised
// `<root>/src` before this split existed, and moving a populated tree to satisfy a naming change is
// exactly the mutation the refusal above exists to prevent.
fn vllm_source_dir() -> NonEmptyStr {
  join([vllm_build_root() as String, "/src"], "") as NonEmptyStr
}
data vllm_source_remote_name: NonEmptyStr = "origin" as NonEmptyStr
data vllm_image_repository: NonEmptyStr = "gunbc-vllm-dsv41-gb10" as NonEmptyStr

// THE GLM BUILD PRODUCES A DIFFERENT RUNTIME AND SAYS SO IN THE REPOSITORY NAME. Sharing one repository
// across two source revisions would make the content-hash tag the only thing distinguishing them, which
// is correct for a cache and wrong for a human reading `docker images` on a serving host at 3am.
data vllm_glm53_image_repository: NonEmptyStr = "gunbc-vllm-glm53-nope-gb10" as NonEmptyStr

// ── WHAT IS BUILT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE FLEET RECIPE IS UPSTREAM'S OWN aarch64 RELEASE RECIPE WITH ONE FIELD CHANGED, and it is written
// that way -- reading each unchanged field off that row rather than re-spelling it -- so the single
// divergence is visible as a divergence. That field is the arch list: upstream's arm64 release builds
// 8.0 through 12.0, and GB10 is 12.1, which is why no published image can carry sm_121 kernels
// however recent its vLLM is.
//
// The parallelism fields are the other difference and they are deliberately NOT part of the build
// identity below; see the key.
data v41_gb10_torch_cuda_arch_list: List<NonEmptyStr> = ["12.1" as NonEmptyStr]

// HOW MUCH OF A HOST A BUILD MAY TAKE IS A POLICY ROW, NOT A MEASUREMENT, and an earlier version of
// this annotation transcribed one. It carried the build host's core count and free memory and
// upstream's documented figure for a different machine, three numbers copied into prose with no
// producer named -- which is the §6 tell exactly, and they rot the moment any of the three changes
// with nobody touching this file.
//
// WHAT IS CITEABLE IS UPSTREAM'S OWN RECIPE, and it is already a declaration:
// extdeps.vllm.source_build's aarch64 release row carries the parallelism upstream passes on a
// machine dedicated to the build. The rows below are this fleet's DEPARTURE from it, chosen low
// because a Spark is not a dedicated builder -- what shares the host is the fleet's business, not
// upstream's -- and they are inputs to a policy rather than readings of anything.
//
// WHAT WOULD RE-DERIVE THE HOST FACTS, if a reader wants them: spark_build_host_reachability_wet
// reaches an admissible host over the modeled route, and a capacity question belongs there as a
// second leg rather than as a sentence here.
data v41_build_max_jobs: Nat = 6

// nvcc_threads COUNTS HARDWARE THREADS, and this row is the fleet's own policy value rather than a
// restatement of upstream's ARG -- so it carries the unit std.measure already models, the same way
// extdeps.ci_runner's published runner sizes carry it for vcpus. The bare Nat stays on
// VllmContainerBuildRecipe, where the field IS upstream's ARG at the cited-spec boundary; the
// conversion happens once, at the call that builds the recipe.
data v41_build_nvcc_threads: HardwareThreadCount = hardware_thread_count(count: 2)

fn v41_gb10_recipe() -> VllmContainerBuildRecipe {
  vllm_recipe_targeting(
    base: vllm_07b75534_aarch64_release_recipe,
    torch_cuda_arch_list: v41_gb10_torch_cuda_arch_list,
    max_jobs: v41_build_max_jobs,
    nvcc_threads: hardware_thread_count_value(t: v41_build_nvcc_threads),
  )
}

// ── WHICH FLASHINFER THIS FLEET BUILDS WITH, WHICH IS A POLICY AND NOT UPSTREAM'S FACT ────────
//
// THE RECIPE'S OWN `flashinfer_version` IS UPSTREAM'S ARG AND STAYS THAT WAY. What this fleet chooses
// to install is a deployment decision, so it sits here rather than inside the cited recipe row --
// DESIGN section 3's split between an upstream interface and the business policy bound to it. The
// pairing is carried as one value so a build cannot be planned with the recipe and the acquisition
// disagreeing about what is going into the image.
type FleetRuntimeBuild sole_constructor {
  recipe: VllmContainerBuildRecipe
  flashinfer: FlashInferAcquisition
}

fn fleet_runtime_build(recipe: VllmContainerBuildRecipe, flashinfer: FlashInferAcquisition) -> FleetRuntimeBuild {
  FleetRuntimeBuild { recipe: recipe, flashinfer: flashinfer }
}

// THE BUILD THAT EXISTS TODAY takes upstream's published wheel, which is what produced the image now
// sitting on spark-a3ee. It is named so the GLM build below reads as a departure from something rather
// than as the only row.
fn v41_gb10_build() -> FleetRuntimeBuild {
  fleet_runtime_build(recipe: v41_gb10_recipe(), flashinfer: flashinfer_0_6_18_post1)
}

// AND THE BUILD GLM-5.3-FLASH NEEDS, which differs in exactly two places and says so: the vLLM source
// revision is vllm#55277's head, which makes the cache writer accept pe_dim 0, and the FlashInfer is a
// source revision because the SM120 NoPE kernel is in no published wheel. Neither half serves without
// the other -- the writer fix clears a guard the decode then refuses at, which is what the executed
// probes established.
fn glm53_nope_recipe() -> VllmContainerBuildRecipe {
  vllm_recipe_targeting(
    base: vllm_glm53_nope_aarch64_recipe,
    torch_cuda_arch_list: v41_gb10_torch_cuda_arch_list,
    max_jobs: v41_build_max_jobs,
    nvcc_threads: hardware_thread_count_value(t: v41_build_nvcc_threads),
  )
}

fn glm53_nope_build() -> FleetRuntimeBuild {
  fleet_runtime_build(recipe: glm53_nope_recipe(), flashinfer: flashinfer_0_7_0_dev20260910)
}

// ── THE DERIVED LAYER, WHICH IS WHERE THE ACQUISITION ACTUALLY LANDS ──────────────────────────
//
// THE BUILD PRODUCES AN IMAGE WITH UPSTREAM'S PINNED FLASHINFER IN IT, because that is what upstream's
// Dockerfile installs and this fleet does not fork that file. The acquisition this deployment needs is
// applied ON TOP, as a second image built FROM the first -- three wheel installs and no compilation,
// because the chosen channel ships prebuilt aarch64 cu130 kernels.
//
// IT IS A SECOND IMAGE AND NOT A SECOND BUILD ARGUMENT, and the distinction is the whole reason this
// exists: upstream's FLASHINFER_VERSION argument varies a version against a hardcoded index, so pointing
// it at a nightly asks the stable index for a wheel that is not there. Installing from the acquisition's
// OWN index is a thing only a layer we author can do.
//
// THE WHEELS ARE NAMED BY FILE AND CHECKED BY DIGEST. A version plus an index is a discovery operation --
// resolve-whatever-is-there-today -- and a nightly index is exactly the kind that reorganises underneath
// one. An installer that fetches different bytes under a name this fleet already decided about should
// fail rather than proceed, which is what the hash is for.
type DerivedRuntimeLayer sole_constructor {
  base_tag: NonEmptyStr
  wheels: List<FlashInferWheel>
  acquisition: FlashInferAcquisition
}

fn glm53_nope_derived_layer(base_tag: NonEmptyStr) -> DerivedRuntimeLayer {
  DerivedRuntimeLayer {
    base_tag: base_tag,
    wheels: flashinfer_0_7_0_dev20260910_wheels,
    acquisition: flashinfer_0_7_0_dev20260910,
  }
}

// AND THE LAYER'S IDENTITY IS THE BASE PLUS WHAT IT INSTALLED. Two derived images over one base with
// different wheels are different runtimes, and a tag that named only the base would let the cache serve
// either for the other.
fn derived_layer_key(l: DerivedRuntimeLayer) -> String {
  join([
    l.base_tag as String, "+",
    flashinfer_acquisition_key(a: l.acquisition), "+",
    join(map(l.wheels, w => w.sha256 as String), ","),
  ], "")
}

// ── THE WHEELS ARE ACQUIRED THROUGH THE AUTHORITY THAT ALREADY OWNS ACQUIRING THINGS ──────────
//
// A SECOND DOWNLOAD-AND-CHECK LOOP IS THE THING NOT TO WRITE. gunbc.artifact_acquisition already owns
// the fold this needs -- observe the destination, use a verified existing copy or fetch from declared
// sources, stage, verify, publish -- and it already separates the outcomes that matter here: a
// destination that is absent, one whose bytes fail their key, one that cannot be read; a source that is
// unavailable, a transfer that was rejected, a verification that failed, a publication that failed.
// Every one of those was about to be re-derived in shell, worse.
//
// STAGING IS THE PART THAT MATTERS MOST FOR A WHEEL. Fetching straight to the install path makes a
// partial or wrong download indistinguishable from a good one on the next run: the file is there, so
// the probe says present, and nothing re-checks it. That is exactly how a 404 page becomes a permanent
// resident of a build cache.
//
// THE SUBJECT IS BUILT FROM A RESOLUTION AND NOT FROM A ROW, because the locator is the thing a row
// cannot carry: the index's href is discovered, and a subject built from a filename would be a
// constructed URL again under a better type.
fn wheel_acquisition_subject(r: WheelResolution, parent: NonEmptyStr) -> AcquisitionSubject? {
  match r {
    WheelResolved { filename: f, locator: loc, expected_sha256: sha } =>
      Present { value: AcquisitionSubject {
        identity: f,
        key: ContentDigestSha256 { hex: sha },
        install_path: join([parent as String, "/", f as String], "") as NonEmptyStr,
        staging_path: Present { value: join([parent as String, "/.staging/", f as String], "") as NonEmptyStr },
        parent_directory: parent,
        sources: [AcquisitionSource {
          identity: join(["index-resolved:", f as String], "") as NonEmptyStr,
          locator: SourceUrl { url: loc },
        }],
      } }
    WheelIndexUnreadable { index: _, cause: _ } => none
    WheelNotInIndex { index: _, filename: _ } => none
    WheelCandidatesConflict { filename: _, locators: _ } => none
  }
}

// AND THE VERIFIED WHEELS ARE RETAINED, WHICH IS A SEPARATE PROPERTY FROM BEING PINNED. A digest pin
// establishes that bytes fetched later are the same bytes; it does nothing about whether they can be
// fetched at all. The channel this deployment depends on is a NIGHTLY one, where withdrawal is ordinary
// rather than exceptional -- so a rebuild that must re-resolve the index is a rebuild that can stop
// working for reasons nobody changed. The install path is under the build root and outlives the build.
fn wheel_retention_directory() -> NonEmptyStr {
  join([vllm_build_root() as String, "/wheels"], "") as NonEmptyStr
}

// ── WHAT A DERIVED LAYER NEEDS BEFORE IT CAN BE BUILT ─────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE POPULATION IS ALL THREE OR IT IS NOT A LAYER. Two of three wheels acquired is not a partial
// success to proceed from: the layer installs them in one pip invocation, and a missing jit-cache means
// the image silently falls back to whatever the base carried -- which is the released build this layer
// exists to replace. So the readiness question is asked of the POPULATION, and its refusal names which
// member is missing rather than reporting a count.
type DerivedLayerInputs
  = DerivedInputsReady { wheels: List<NonEmptyStr> }
  | DerivedInputsIncomplete { missing: List<NonEmptyStr>, acquired: List<NonEmptyStr> }

// THE JOIN IS ON IDENTITY AND THE RESULT CARRIES THE PATH. An earlier revision took `acquired` as a list
// of bare strings and compared them against required FILENAMES, while the acquisition fold returns
// INSTALL PATHS -- so the comparison was between `/home/.../wheels/x.whl` and `x.whl` and matched nothing.
// A complete acquisition reported every wheel missing, and the diagnostic named the correct filenames,
// which is what would have made it slow to find.
fn derived_layer_inputs(required: List<FlashInferWheel>, acquired: List<AcquiredWheel>) -> DerivedLayerInputs {
  let missing = filter(required, w => !any(acquired, a => (a.filename as String) == (w.filename as String)))
  if count(missing) == 0 {
    DerivedInputsReady { wheels: map(required, w => w.filename) }
  } else {
    DerivedInputsIncomplete { missing: map(missing, w => w.filename), acquired: map(acquired, a => a.filename) }
  }
}

// ── THE DERIVED LAYER AS A GENERATED FILE, WHICH IS A PROJECTION AND NOT A SCRIPT ───────────
//
// THE MODEL ALREADY HOLDS EVERY FACT THIS FILE CONTAINS -- the base, the wheel population, the install
// operation and its order -- so the Dockerfile is a rendering of rows rather than a second place those
// decisions are made. What must not happen is the shape this repository keeps paying for: a hand-written
// build file beside the rows it was transcribed from, agreeing with them on the day it was typed.
//
// THE BASE IS BOUND BY OBSERVED CONTENT AND NOT BY ITS TAG. `DerivedRuntimeLayer` carries a base_tag,
// which is what a person types and what a person can move. A tag read at planning time and a tag read by
// the build are two reads of a mutable name, so the identity is observed through the privileged leg and
// the generated FROM carries the local image id that read returned.
//
// RUN IS EXEC FORM, AND A WORD THIS GENERATOR CANNOT SPELL REFUSES RATHER THAN BEING ESCAPED. Shell form
// hands the install argv to /bin/sh on the far side, which is a second quoting authority over a list this
// module already holds exactly; the JSON array carries the words as words. A word containing a quote or a
// backslash is refused, because escaping is the point where a generated file starts disagreeing with the
// list it was generated from -- and a wheel filename is upstream data, not a literal authored here.
//
// THE CONSUMER THAT EXECUTES THIS LANDS NEXT AND ITS TRIGGER IS NAMED: the derived build leg needs the
// administrator credential the privileged leg takes (GUNBC_SPARK_BOOTSTRAP_CREDENTIAL_FILE), without
// which no docker operation on a Spark can run at all -- gunbc-automation holds NOPASSWD sudo for exactly
// two commands and is in no docker group (measured on srv9, 2026-09-12). The rendering below is complete
// and executed by its fixtures; the leg that hands its bytes to a build host is not written, because
// nothing in this session could have run it.
fn derived_layer_image_wheel_dir() -> NonEmptyStr {
  "/tmp/gunbc-wheels" as NonEmptyStr
}

fn dockerfile_json_word(w: String) -> String? {
  if string_contains(s: w, pattern: "\"") || string_contains(s: w, pattern: "\\") {
    none
  } else {
    Present { value: join(["\"", w, "\""], "") }
  }
}

fn dockerfile_exec_run_line(argv: List<String>) -> String? {
  let quoted = flat_map(argv, w => match dockerfile_json_word(w: w) {
    Present { value: q } => [q]
    Absent => []
  })
  if count(quoted) != count(argv) {
    none
  } else {
    Present { value: join(["RUN [", join(quoted, ", "), "]\n"], "") }
  }
}

type DerivedLayerRender
  = DerivedLayerRendered { content: String }
  | DerivedLayerRenderRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }

// THE INSTALL PATHS ARE ABSOLUTE INSIDE THE IMAGE, because a bare filename resolves against a working
// directory this generator never set, and the one upstream chose is not a fact this module holds.
// THE INSTALL IS ORDERED AND THE ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING. flashinfer-python is installed with its
// dependencies already satisfied by the base image, so it goes in with --no-deps: letting it resolve
// freely is exactly how a fresh dependency solve reaches back to the stable index and restores the
// released build this layer exists to replace. The jit-cache wheel is last because it is the one whose
// filename carries the architecture, and a wrong one there is a silent mismatch rather than a refusal.
fn derived_layer_image_install_argv(l: DerivedRuntimeLayer) -> List<String> {
  concat(
    ["python3", "-m", "pip", "install", "--no-deps", "--force-reinstall"],
    map(l.wheels, w => join([derived_layer_image_wheel_dir() as String, "/", w.filename as String], "")),
  )
}

fn render_derived_layer_dockerfile(base_image_id: NonEmptyStr, l: DerivedRuntimeLayer) -> DerivedLayerRender {
  match dockerfile_exec_run_line(argv: derived_layer_image_install_argv(l: l)) {
    Absent => DerivedLayerRenderRefused {
      cause: "a wheel filename carries a quote or a backslash, which this generator refuses to escape" as NonEmptyStr,
    }
    Present { value: run_line } =>
      DerivedLayerRendered {
        content: join([
          "FROM ", base_image_id as String, "\n",
          join(map(l.wheels, w => join([
            "COPY ", w.filename as String, " ",
            derived_layer_image_wheel_dir() as String, "/", w.filename as String, "\n",
          ], "")), ""),
          run_line,
        ], ""),
      }
  }
}

// ── GETTING THE IMAGE TO THE HOSTS THAT SERVE IT, WHICH NOTHING MODELLED ──────────────────────
//
// THE BUILD HOST IS NOT A SERVING HOST AND THIS REPOSITORY HAD NO WORD FOR THE DIFFERENCE. Grepping the
// corpus for an image export or import returns nothing: every runtime row names an image as though
// naming it put it where it is needed. For a registry-published image that is nearly true; for one built
// locally and never pushed it is not true at all, and the fleet's existing V4.1 image is exactly that
// case -- it exists on one host and the model cannot say so.
//
// AN ARCHIVE IS A THIRD IDENTITY AND MUST NOT BE CONFLATED WITH THE OTHER TWO. A build request key says
// what was asked for; a local image configuration digest says what the daemon holds; an exported
// archive's sha256 says what travelled. They are related by the operations between them and by nothing
// else, and a transfer receipt that reported the archive digest as the image identity would be naming
// the envelope for the letter.
type ImageDistributionOutcome
  = ImagePresentOnHost { host: HostIdentity, local_image_id: NonEmptyStr }
  | ImageLoadedOnHost { host: HostIdentity, local_image_id: NonEmptyStr, archive_sha256: NonEmptyStr }
  | ImageTransferRefused { host: HostIdentity, step: NonEmptyStr, cause: String }
  | ImageIdentityMismatch { host: HostIdentity, expected: NonEmptyStr, observed: NonEmptyStr }

// THE DESTINATION IS READ BACK RATHER THAN INFERRED FROM A SUCCESSFUL TRANSFER. `docker load` reporting
// success says an archive was accepted; it does not say which image id the daemon now holds under the
// tag this deployment will launch. Those differ whenever a tag already existed on the target -- which is
// the ordinary case on a host that has served a previous runtime, and precisely when a stale image would
// be hardest to notice.
fn distribution_outcome_ready(o: ImageDistributionOutcome) -> Bool {
  match o {
    ImagePresentOnHost { host: _, local_image_id: _ } => true
    ImageLoadedOnHost { host: _, local_image_id: _, archive_sha256: _ } => true
    ImageTransferRefused { host: _, step: _, cause: _ } => false
    ImageIdentityMismatch { host: _, expected: _, observed: _ } => false
  }
}

// ── THE POPULATION IS THE UNIT, BECAUSE A TENSOR-PARALLEL ARM IS ────────────────────────────────
//
// THREE HOSTS WITH THE IMAGE DO NOT SATISFY A FOUR-HOST DEPLOYMENT, and the failure mode of pretending
// otherwise is specific and expensive: the engine starts, ranks join, and the missing host fails when
// its container is created -- after the other three have loaded weights. The distribution question is
// therefore asked of the whole participant roster and answers with the members that are NOT ready,
// rather than with a count that a caller has to compare against something.
type ImagePopulationStanding
  = ImageAvailableEverywhere { hosts: List<HostIdentity>, image_id: NonEmptyStr }
  | ImageMissingOnMembers { not_ready: List<ImageDistributionOutcome>, ready_count: Nat }

fn image_population_standing(outcomes: List<ImageDistributionOutcome>, image_id: NonEmptyStr) -> ImagePopulationStanding {
  let not_ready = filter(outcomes, o => !distribution_outcome_ready(o: o))
  if count(not_ready) == 0 {
    ImageAvailableEverywhere {
      hosts: fold(outcomes, init: [], f: (acc, o) => match o {
        ImagePresentOnHost { host: h, local_image_id: _ } => concat(acc, [h])
        ImageLoadedOnHost { host: h, local_image_id: _, archive_sha256: _ } => concat(acc, [h])
        ImageTransferRefused { host: _, step: _, cause: _ } => acc
        ImageIdentityMismatch { host: _, expected: _, observed: _ } => acc
      }),
      image_id: image_id,
    }
  } else {
    ImageMissingOnMembers { not_ready: not_ready, ready_count: count(outcomes) - count(not_ready) }
  }
}

// AND AN EMPTY ROSTER IS NOT AVAILABILITY. A fold over no hosts finds no failures, which would report a
// deployment ready on a population nobody named -- the vacuous-truth shape this corpus has paid for
// before. The roster is the deployment's participant list, so an empty one is a caller defect.
//
// THE ROSTER IS A HOST POPULATION AND NOT A COUNT, which is the correction that matters here. An earlier
// revision took `expected_members: Nat` and compared it against `count(outcomes)`, so four ready outcomes
// satisfied a four-host arm REGARDLESS OF WHICH HOSTS THEY WERE ABOUT: four probes of one host, or of
// three required hosts and a bystander, both answer true. A count is not a membership test, and the state
// it admits is exactly the one the annotation above says is expensive -- an engine that starts and fails
// when the host nobody actually checked creates its container.
//
// SO EVERY REQUIRED IDENTITY MUST BE MATCHED BY EXACTLY ONE READY OUTCOME CARRYING THE EXPECTED IMAGE. A
// required host with no outcome is missing; a required host with two is ambiguous rather than doubly
// ready, because two outcomes about one host can disagree and nothing here would choose between them; and
// an outcome about a host nobody required is not evidence for any host that was.
fn outcome_ready_for_image(o: ImageDistributionOutcome, image_id: NonEmptyStr) -> Bool {
  match o {
    ImagePresentOnHost { host: _, local_image_id: id } => (id as String) == (image_id as String)
    ImageLoadedOnHost { host: _, local_image_id: id, archive_sha256: _ } => (id as String) == (image_id as String)
    ImageTransferRefused { host: _, step: _, cause: _ } => false
    ImageIdentityMismatch { host: _, expected: _, observed: _ } => false
  }
}

fn distribution_outcome_host(o: ImageDistributionOutcome) -> HostIdentity {
  match o {
    ImagePresentOnHost { host: h, local_image_id: _ } => h
    ImageLoadedOnHost { host: h, local_image_id: _, archive_sha256: _ } => h
    ImageTransferRefused { host: h, step: _, cause: _ } => h
    ImageIdentityMismatch { host: h, expected: _, observed: _ } => h
  }
}

fn image_available_for_arm(outcomes: List<ImageDistributionOutcome>, image_id: NonEmptyStr, required: List<HostIdentity>) -> Bool {
  if count(required) == 0 { false }
  else {
    all(required, r =>
      count(filter(outcomes, o =>
        host_identity_eq(a: distribution_outcome_host(o: o), b: r) && outcome_ready_for_image(o: o, image_id: image_id))) == 1)
  }
}

// ── WHAT MUST BE TRUE OF THE FINAL IMAGE, ASKED OF THE IMAGE AND NOT OF THE BUILD ─────────────
//
// A BUILD THAT SUCCEEDED IS NOT AN IMAGE THAT CARRIES WHAT WAS ASKED FOR. vLLM's own installation step
// resolves requirements/cuda.txt, which pins flashinfer-python against the stable index; a derived layer
// that installs the nightly and then triggers any further dependency resolution can have the pinned
// wheel quietly replaced by the released one it was chosen to escape. The image would look correct, the
// build log would read clean, and the kernel that refuses NoPE would be the one loaded.
//
// SO THE CHECK IS EXECUTED INSIDE THE FINAL IMAGE and asks three separate questions: which FlashInfer
// version is importable, whether the SM120 NoPE symbol is present in the installed bytes, and which vLLM
// build the compiled extension actually came from. Each has its own arm, because "the wheel was replaced"
// and "the extension is the old one" are different repairs.
type FinalImageInspection
  = FinalImageCarriesAcquisition { flashinfer_version: NonEmptyStr, vllm_version: NonEmptyStr }
  | FinalImageDependencyReplaced { observed: NonEmptyStr, expected: NonEmptyStr }
  | FinalImageExtensionStale { observed: NonEmptyStr, expected: NonEmptyStr }
  | FinalImageUnread { cause: NonEmptyStr }

// ── WHETHER A BUILD CAN BE PERFORMED AT ALL, ASKED BEFORE AN HOUR IS SPENT ────────────────────
//
// A BUILD THAT CANNOT INSTALL ITS OWN DEPENDENCY MUST REFUSE BEFORE IT STARTS, not discover it at the
// pip step seventy minutes in. The cold build of this image took about that long, on a host taken out
// of service to do it, so the difference between refusing here and failing there is a working day.
//
// AND THE REFUSAL NAMES THE MISSING CAPABILITY RATHER THAN THE SYMPTOM. `RuntimeBuildNeedsDerivedDockerfile`
// is not "the build failed" -- it is "upstream's Dockerfile installs a published wheel and this
// acquisition is a git revision", which is a statement about what must be authored before any build of
// this shape can run.
type RuntimeBuildAdmission
  = RuntimeBuildAdmitted { key_input: String }
  | RuntimeBuildNeedsDerivedDockerfile { needs: NonEmptyStr, acquisition: String }
  | RuntimeBuildDependencyCannotServe { cause: NonEmptyStr }

fn admit_runtime_build(b: FleetRuntimeBuild) -> RuntimeBuildAdmission {
  if !flashinfer_reachable_by_upstream_dockerfile(a: b.flashinfer) {
    RuntimeBuildNeedsDerivedDockerfile {
      needs: flashinfer_redirected_index_missing_capability,
      acquisition: flashinfer_acquisition_key(a: b.flashinfer),
    }
  } else {
    RuntimeBuildAdmitted { key_input: flashinfer_acquisition_key(a: b.flashinfer) }
  }
}

// AND WHETHER THE RESULTING IMAGE COULD SERVE THE GEOMETRY, which is a different question from whether
// it can be built: the 0.6.18 build succeeds and produces an image whose SM120 backend refuses NoPE.
// Asking them separately is what keeps a green build from being read as a serving runtime.
fn runtime_build_serves_glm53_nope(b: FleetRuntimeBuild) -> Bool {
  sm120_nope_is_supported(s: flashinfer_sm120_nope_support(a: b.flashinfer))
}

fn vllm_recipe_arch_is_buildable(r: VllmContainerBuildRecipe) -> Bool {
  fold(r.torch_cuda_arch_list, init: true, f: (acc, a) => acc && vllm_cuda_arch_supported(arch: a))
}

// ── THE IDENTITY OF THE BUILD ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A CONTENT KEY OVER THE INPUTS, AND THE TAG IS THAT KEY. The image this lane produces is a
// materialization: given the same source revision, the same bases, the same toolchain pins and the
// same arch list, it is the same artifact, and given a different one it is a different artifact that
// must not be able to wear the same name. So the tag is DERIVED from the key rather than chosen, and
// a reader who wants to know whether two images were built from the same inputs compares two strings
// instead of reading two build logs.
//
// WHAT THE KEY IS OVER, STATED SO IT IS NOT MISREAD. It is over the REQUEST -- the inputs as
// declared -- and two of those inputs are MUTABLE REFERENCES rather than content identities:
// BUILD_BASE_IMAGE and FINAL_BASE_IMAGE are registry TAGS, and a tag can be repointed at different
// bytes without the string changing. So two builds sharing a key are two builds that asked for the
// same thing, which is weaker than two builds that consumed the same bytes.
//
// 🟡 dissolve-on: resolve both base references to their manifest digests through the registry API at
// build time and key on those instead of on the tags, which makes the key a content identity end to
// end. The resolution is a plain registry lookup -- this lane already performs one for the published
// image -- so the trigger is a modeled registry-resolution operation, not new information.
//
// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT IN THE KEY. max_jobs and nvcc_threads are how much of the host the build
// was allowed to use, not what it produced; folding them in would mint a fresh identity every time
// the machine's spare capacity changed, and each fresh identity is a cache miss and a rebuild of
// something already built. That is the redundancy DESIGN §2 prices, bought for nothing: the compiler
// output does not depend on how many compilers ran at once.

fn vllm_image_build_key_inputs(r: VllmContainerBuildRecipe) -> List<ArtifactRealizationInput> {
  [
    ArtifactRealizationInput { identity: "source_repository" as NonEmptyStr, content_digest: content_hash_atom(value: r.source.repository) },
    ArtifactRealizationInput { identity: "source_revision" as NonEmptyStr, content_digest: content_hash_atom(value: r.source.full_revision) },
    ArtifactRealizationInput { identity: "dockerfile_path" as NonEmptyStr, content_digest: content_hash_atom(value: r.dockerfile_path) },
    ArtifactRealizationInput { identity: "build_target" as NonEmptyStr, content_digest: content_hash_atom(value: r.build_target) },
    ArtifactRealizationInput { identity: "cuda_version" as NonEmptyStr, content_digest: content_hash_atom(value: r.cuda_version) },
    ArtifactRealizationInput { identity: "build_base_image" as NonEmptyStr, content_digest: content_hash_atom(value: r.build_base_image) },
    ArtifactRealizationInput { identity: "final_base_image" as NonEmptyStr, content_digest: content_hash_atom(value: r.final_base_image) },
    ArtifactRealizationInput { identity: "python_version" as NonEmptyStr, content_digest: content_hash_atom(value: r.python_version) },
    ArtifactRealizationInput { identity: "torch_version" as NonEmptyStr, content_digest: content_hash_atom(value: r.torch_version) },
    ArtifactRealizationInput { identity: "flashinfer_version" as NonEmptyStr, content_digest: content_hash_atom(value: r.flashinfer_version) },
    ArtifactRealizationInput { identity: "torch_cuda_arch_list" as NonEmptyStr, content_digest: content_hash_atom(value: vllm_torch_cuda_arch_list_wire(archs: r.torch_cuda_arch_list) as NonEmptyStr) },
  ]
}

// THE KEY IS MINTED BY std.artifact_store, NOT BY A FOLD WRITTEN HERE. An earlier revision combined
// the input atoms with its own content_hash fold, which is a second spelling of
// artifact_realization_digest -- the authority that already exists for "a list of named realization
// inputs, folded into one content identity". Inhabiting it also buys the thing the hand fold did not
// have: every input carries its NAME, so two recipes that differ only by which field holds a value
// cannot collide, and a reader sees which inputs the key covers without counting positions.
// THE RELEASE PIN ALONE WAS NEVER A COMPLETE KEY FOR THIS INPUT, and the gap was invisible while only
// one acquisition existed. `flashinfer_version` keys the recipe's declared string; the acquisition keys
// how the dependency is actually OBTAINED, and a release and a source revision can spell similar
// versions while installing entirely different bytes. Both belong in the key.
fn fleet_build_key_inputs(b: FleetRuntimeBuild) -> List<ArtifactRealizationInput> {
  concat(
    vllm_image_build_key_inputs(r: b.recipe),
    [ArtifactRealizationInput {
      identity: "flashinfer_acquisition" as NonEmptyStr,
      content_digest: content_hash_atom(value: flashinfer_acquisition_key(a: b.flashinfer) as NonEmptyStr),
    }],
  )
}

fn vllm_image_build_key(r: VllmContainerBuildRecipe) -> ContentHash {
  artifact_realization_digest(inputs: vllm_image_build_key_inputs(r: r))
}

fn vllm_image_tag(r: VllmContainerBuildRecipe) -> NonEmptyStr {
  join([vllm_image_repository as String, ":", serialize_content_hash(hash: vllm_image_build_key(r: r)) as String], "") as NonEmptyStr
}

// ── THE INVOCATION ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE BUILD ARGUMENTS ARE THE ONES UPSTREAM'S Dockerfile PUBLISHES, spelled exactly as its ARGs
// spell them. FINAL_BASE_IMAGE is not passed and that is deliberate: its ARG default is
// nvidia/cuda:${CUDA_VERSION}-base-ubuntu24.04, so passing CUDA_VERSION already determines it, and
// the recipe row records the value it resolves to. RUN_WHEEL_CHECK disables only upstream's PyPI
// publication-size guard, which is a rule about what may be uploaded to PyPI and has nothing to say
// about an image nobody publishes.
fn vllm_image_build_args(r: VllmContainerBuildRecipe) -> List<DockerBuildArg> {
  [
    DockerBuildArg { name: "CUDA_VERSION" as NonEmptyStr, value: r.cuda_version as String },
    DockerBuildArg { name: "BUILD_BASE_IMAGE" as NonEmptyStr, value: r.build_base_image as String },
    DockerBuildArg { name: "torch_cuda_arch_list" as NonEmptyStr, value: vllm_torch_cuda_arch_list_wire(archs: r.torch_cuda_arch_list) },
    DockerBuildArg { name: "max_jobs" as NonEmptyStr, value: to_string(r.max_jobs) },
    DockerBuildArg { name: "nvcc_threads" as NonEmptyStr, value: to_string(r.nvcc_threads) },
    DockerBuildArg { name: "RUN_WHEEL_CHECK" as NonEmptyStr, value: "false" },
  ]
}

// ── THE ARGUMENT THAT WAS KEYED AND NEVER PASSED ──────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `flashinfer_version` SAT IN THE BUILD KEY AND IN NO BUILD ARGUMENT, which is worse than either
// alternative. The Dockerfile defaults FLASHINFER_VERSION to 0.6.18, so the recipe's field changed the
// content hash and nothing else: two recipes naming different FlashInfer versions would have produced
// byte-identical images under two different keys, and the cache would have served whichever was asked
// for first. A field that keys a result it cannot affect is a fork between the identity of an artifact
// and the artifact.
//
// IT IS PASSED NOW, AND ONLY THE ARM THAT CAN BE PASSED. A source revision has no spelling this
// argument accepts, so it contributes no argument here and is refused upstream of this function by
// `admit_runtime_build` -- rather than silently falling back to the Dockerfile default, which is the
// shape that hid the defect in the first place.
fn fleet_build_args(b: FleetRuntimeBuild) -> List<DockerBuildArg> {
  concat(
    vllm_image_build_args(r: b.recipe),
    match flashinfer_published_version(a: b.flashinfer) {
      Present { value: v } => [DockerBuildArg { name: "FLASHINFER_VERSION" as NonEmptyStr, value: v as String }]
      Absent => []
    },
  )
}

fn vllm_image_dockerfile_path(r: VllmContainerBuildRecipe) -> NonEmptyStr {
  join([vllm_source_dir() as String, "/", r.dockerfile_path as String], "") as NonEmptyStr
}

fn vllm_image_build_spec(r: VllmContainerBuildRecipe) -> DockerBuildSpec {
  DockerBuildSpec {
    tag: vllm_image_tag(r: r),
    context_dir: vllm_source_dir() as FilePath,
    dockerfile: vllm_image_dockerfile_path(r: r),
    target: r.build_target,
    platform: "linux/arm64" as NonEmptyStr,
    build_args: vllm_image_build_args(r: r),
  }
}

// THE SPEC A FLEET BUILD EXECUTES, WHICH IS THE ONE THAT CARRIES THE ACQUISITION. `fleet_build_args` was
// added and then consumed by nothing: the executed spec kept calling `vllm_image_build_args`, so the
// FlashInfer argument existed as a renderer and reached no build. That is the dangling declaration DESIGN
// section 3c names, authored in the same change that added the vocabulary for catching it.
//
// THE BASE SPEC STAYS AS IT IS, because it is what upstream's Dockerfile can express. This one is the
// fleet's, and the derived layer is where an acquisition upstream's ARG cannot reach actually lands.
fn fleet_image_build_spec(b: FleetRuntimeBuild) -> DockerBuildSpec {
  DockerBuildSpec {
    tag: vllm_image_tag(r: b.recipe),
    context_dir: vllm_source_dir() as FilePath,
    dockerfile: vllm_image_dockerfile_path(r: b.recipe),
    target: b.recipe.build_target,
    platform: "linux/arm64" as NonEmptyStr,
    build_args: fleet_build_args(b: b),
  }
}

// ── THE LEGS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

type ImageLegOutcome
  = ImageLegRan { stdout: String }
  | ImageLegFailed { step: NonEmptyStr, cause: String }

// DOCKER DOES NOT GO OVER THIS LEG, AND AN EARLIER REVISION SENT IT THERE ANYWAY. The key-authenticated
// leg below runs as the managed executor, and this PR's own recorded facts say that account is in no
// docker group on any Spark -- so every docker leg it carried would have authenticated and then been
// refused at the socket. It did not, once, on srv5: that host has a hand-installed key for the human
// briansrls AND that account in the docker group, which is provisioning drift no modeled route
// produces. The fallback build ran because of that drift, and recording it is the point -- an
// unaccounted-for state was load-bearing for a whole lane.
//
// So the docker legs bind to gunbc.spark.model_snapshot_materialize spark_remote_run, the one route
// the corpus models for that daemon, and this leg keeps the git work, which needs no root.
func vllm_docker_leg(ctx: SparkMaterializeContext, step: NonEmptyStr, command: ArgvCommand) -> ImageLegOutcome
  uses net: Network
{
  match spark_remote_run(ctx: ctx, argv: argv_words(command: command)) {
    RemoteUnreachable { cause: why } => ImageLegFailed { step: step, cause: why }
    RemoteRan { exit_code: code, stdout: out, stderr: err } =>
      if code != 0 {
        ImageLegFailed { step: step, cause: join(["exit=", to_string(code), " stderr=", trim(s: err)], "") }
      } else {
        ImageLegRan { stdout: trim(s: out) }
      }
  }
}

fn vllm_image_leg(target: SshTarget, context: FleetSshExecutionContext, step: NonEmptyStr, command: ArgvCommand) -> ImageLegOutcome {
  match typed_argv_exec_over_fleet_ssh(target: target, context: context, argv: argv_words(command: command)) {
    TypedArgvExecRefused { reason: why } => ImageLegFailed { step: step, cause: why }
    TypedArgvExecConverged { result: r } =>
      if r.success {
        ImageLegRan { stdout: trim(s: r.stdout) }
      } else {
        ImageLegFailed { step: step, cause: join(["exit=", to_string(r.exit_code), " stderr=", trim(s: r.stderr)], "") }
      }
  }
}

// SOURCE MATERIALIZATION IS CONVERGENT, AND THE READBACK COMES FIRST. A tree already sitting at the
// requested commit needs nothing done to it, and a clone into an existing directory is an error
// rather than a no-op -- so the question "is HEAD already this revision" is asked before anything is
// created. It is also the only honest completion test: a source directory that EXISTS says nothing
// about which commit is checked out in it.
type VllmSourceState
  = VllmSourceAtRevision { revision: NonEmptyStr, already_present: Bool }
  | VllmSourceRefused { step: NonEmptyStr, cause: String }

// HEAD IS NOT THE TREE, AND A BUILD STAMPED WITH A REVISION IT DID NOT BUILD IS THE FABRICATION §5
// FORBIDS. `rev-parse HEAD` answers which commit is checked out; it says nothing about a modified
// working tree, so a context with local edits would produce an image whose recorded provenance names
// an unmodified upstream revision. The porcelain status is empty exactly when the tree matches, so
// this is a readback rather than a parse, and a dirty context REFUSES rather than building something
// no one can reproduce.
fn vllm_source_is_clean(stdout: String) -> Bool {
  trim(s: stdout) == ""
}

// ── A FAILED READ IS NOT AN ABSENCE, AND THE CONVERGER ACTED ON THE DIFFERENCE ────────────────
//
// `vllm_source_head` RETURNED AN OPTIONAL AND MAPPED EVERY FAILURE TO `none`, which the converger then
// read as "no tree here, clone one". A directory that exists and cannot be read -- wrong owner, a
// permission change, a half-finished clone, a host that answered but whose git is missing -- became a
// materialisation. In the best case the clone then fails on a non-empty directory; in the worse one it
// succeeds into a tree somebody else was using.
//
// THE STATES ARE NAMED SEPARATELY BECAUSE THEY OWE DIFFERENT WORK. Absent licenses materialisation and
// nothing else. Unreadable is an access repair and licenses nothing. A revision mismatch is a refusal,
// not a checkout. A dirty tree refuses because an image stamped with a revision it did not build from
// is the fabrication DESIGN section 5 forbids. Only ready licenses a build.
type VllmSourceStanding
  = VllmSourceAbsent
  | VllmSourceUnreadable { cause: String }
  | VllmSourceAtOtherRevision { observed: String, wanted: NonEmptyStr }
  | VllmSourceDirty { detail: String }
  | VllmSourceReady { revision: NonEmptyStr }

// THE DIRECTORY QUESTION IS ASKED WITH `stat` AND NOT WITH A SHELL SCRIPT, which is what keeps this
// inside the corpus's one rule about commands: ArgvCommand is sole_constructor and every admitted
// caller is a named builder for ONE operation of ONE tool, homed in that tool's own extdeps module.
// A minted `sh -c` here would be a second command authority answering a question `stat` already
// answers -- and `%F` distinguishes exactly the two cases that matter, because a path that is not
// there makes stat exit non-zero while a path that is there names its type.
//
// THE ORDER IS DELIBERATE: existence first, git second. `git rev-parse` fails identically for a
// missing directory and an unreadable one, so asking it first would collapse the distinction this
// type exists to draw.
func observe_vllm_source(target: SshTarget, context: FleetSshExecutionContext, source: VllmSourceRevision) -> VllmSourceStanding
  uses net: Network
{
  let dir = vllm_source_dir_for(source: source) as String
  match vllm_image_leg(target: target, context: context, step: "source-exists" as NonEmptyStr, command: stat_file_type_command(path: dir)) {
    ImageLegFailed { step: _, cause: _ } => VllmSourceAbsent
    ImageLegRan { stdout: kind } =>
      if trim(s: kind) != "directory" {
        VllmSourceUnreadable { cause: join([dir, " exists and is a ", trim(s: kind), " rather than a directory"], "") }
      } else {
      match vllm_image_leg(target: target, context: context, step: "source-head" as NonEmptyStr, command: git_rev_parse_head_command(repository_dir: dir)) {
        ImageLegFailed { step: _, cause: why } => VllmSourceUnreadable { cause: why }
        ImageLegRan { stdout: out } =>
        if trim(s: out) != (source.full_revision as String) {
          VllmSourceAtOtherRevision { observed: trim(s: out), wanted: source.full_revision }
        } else {
          match vllm_image_leg(target: target, context: context, step: "source-clean" as NonEmptyStr, command: git_status_porcelain_command(repository_dir: dir)) {
            ImageLegFailed { step: _, cause: why } => VllmSourceUnreadable { cause: why }
            ImageLegRan { stdout: st } =>
              if trim(s: st) == "" { VllmSourceReady { revision: source.full_revision } }
              else { VllmSourceDirty { detail: trim(s: st) } }
          }
        }
      }
      }
  }
}

// ONLY ABSENCE LICENSES MATERIALISATION AND ONLY READINESS LICENSES A BUILD. Two predicates rather than
// one standing-to-Bool, because the two questions have different answers for three of the five arms.
fn source_licenses_materialization(st: VllmSourceStanding) -> Bool {
  match st {
    VllmSourceAbsent => true
    VllmSourceUnreadable { cause: _ } => false
    VllmSourceAtOtherRevision { observed: _, wanted: _ } => false
    VllmSourceDirty { detail: _ } => false
    VllmSourceReady { revision: _ } => false
  }
}

fn source_licenses_build(st: VllmSourceStanding) -> Bool {
  match st {
    VllmSourceReady { revision: _ } => true
    VllmSourceAbsent => false
    VllmSourceUnreadable { cause: _ } => false
    VllmSourceAtOtherRevision { observed: _, wanted: _ } => false
    VllmSourceDirty { detail: _ } => false
  }
}

fn vllm_source_head(target: SshTarget, context: FleetSshExecutionContext) -> String? {
  match vllm_image_leg(target: target, context: context, step: "source-head" as NonEmptyStr, command: git_rev_parse_head_command(repository_dir: vllm_source_dir() as String)) {
    ImageLegFailed { step: _, cause: _ } => none
    ImageLegRan { stdout: head } => Present { value: head }
  }
}

fn vllm_materialize_source_steps(r: VllmContainerBuildRecipe) -> List<ArgvCommand> {
  [
    mkdir_parents_command_at(mkdir_binary: mkdir_path_resolved_program, path: vllm_build_root() as String),
    git_clone_shallow_command(repository: vllm_clone_url(source: r.source), destination: vllm_source_dir() as String),
    git_fetch_shallow_revision_command(repository_dir: vllm_source_dir() as String, remote: vllm_source_remote_name as String, revision: r.source.full_revision as String),
    git_checkout_revision_command(repository_dir: vllm_source_dir() as String, revision: r.source.full_revision as String),
  ]
}

fn vllm_image_run_steps(target: SshTarget, context: FleetSshExecutionContext, step: NonEmptyStr, commands: List<ArgvCommand>) -> ImageLegOutcome? {
  fold(commands, init: none, f: (acc, c) =>
    match acc {
      Present { value: failure } => Present { value: failure }
      Absent =>
        match vllm_image_leg(target: target, context: context, step: step, command: c) {
          ImageLegRan { stdout: _ } => none
          ImageLegFailed { step: s, cause: why } => Present { value: ImageLegFailed { step: s, cause: why } }
        }
    })
}

func vllm_source_tree_clean(target: SshTarget, context: FleetSshExecutionContext) -> VllmSourceState?
  uses net: Network
{
  match vllm_image_leg(target: target, context: context, step: "source-clean" as NonEmptyStr, command: git_status_porcelain_command(repository_dir: vllm_source_dir() as String)) {
    ImageLegFailed { step: st, cause: why } =>
      Present { value: VllmSourceRefused { step: st, cause: join(["the working-tree status could not be read, so nothing is known about whether the context matches its revision: ", why], "") } }
    ImageLegRan { stdout: status } =>
      if vllm_source_is_clean(stdout: status) {
        none
      } else {
        Present { value: VllmSourceRefused {
          step: "source-clean" as NonEmptyStr,
          cause: join(["the source tree at ", vllm_source_dir() as String, " has local modifications, so an image built from it would be stamped with a revision it was not built from:\n", trim(s: status)], ""),
        } }
      }
  }
}

func converge_vllm_source(target: SshTarget, context: FleetSshExecutionContext, r: VllmContainerBuildRecipe) -> VllmSourceState
  uses net: Network
{
  match vllm_source_head(target: target, context: context) {
    Present { value: head } =>
      if head != (r.source.full_revision as String) {
        VllmSourceRefused {
          step: "source-head" as NonEmptyStr,
          cause: join(["the source tree at ", vllm_source_dir() as String, " is checked out at ", head, " and this build wants ", r.source.full_revision as String, "; it is not moved from under a build that may be reading it"], ""),
        }
      } else {
        match vllm_source_tree_clean(target: target, context: context) {
          Present { value: refusal } => refusal
          Absent => VllmSourceAtRevision { revision: r.source.full_revision, already_present: true }
        }
      }
    Absent =>
      match vllm_image_run_steps(target: target, context: context, step: "source-materialize" as NonEmptyStr, commands: vllm_materialize_source_steps(r: r)) {
        Present { value: failure } =>
          match failure {
            ImageLegFailed { step: st, cause: why } => VllmSourceRefused { step: st, cause: why }
            ImageLegRan { stdout: _ } => VllmSourceRefused { step: "source-materialize" as NonEmptyStr, cause: "unreachable: a successful leg is never recorded as a failure" }
          }
        Absent =>
          match vllm_source_head(target: target, context: context) {
            Absent => VllmSourceRefused { step: "source-readback" as NonEmptyStr, cause: "the tree was materialized and HEAD still could not be read" }
            Present { value: head } =>
              if head != (r.source.full_revision as String) {
                VllmSourceRefused { step: "source-readback" as NonEmptyStr, cause: join(["checkout reported success and HEAD reads ", head], "") }
              } else {
                match vllm_source_tree_clean(target: target, context: context) {
                  Present { value: refusal } => refusal
                  Absent => VllmSourceAtRevision { revision: r.source.full_revision, already_present: false }
                }
              }
          }
      }
  }
}

// ── THE IMAGE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE IDENTITY READ BACK IS THE IMAGE CONFIG DIGEST, NOT A REGISTRY DIGEST, and the difference is
// stated rather than glossed. A registry digest is the digest of a manifest a registry accepted, and
// this image is deliberately not pushed anywhere; what `docker image inspect --format {{.Id}}`
// returns is the sha256 of the image configuration, which is a content identity of the built image on
// this host and is what a `docker run` can be pinned to here. Calling it a registry digest would
// claim a publication that did not happen.
// WHAT PRODUCED THESE BYTES IS NOT THE SAME QUESTION AS WHAT WE ASKED FOR, and an earlier revision
// answered the second while recording the first. `built_from` and `build_key` are copied from the
// REQUESTED recipe, so an image that merely carries the expected tag -- retagged by hand, left over
// from another run, built from a different context -- was returned as though this lane had built it
// from that revision. The tag is a name, and a name is not provenance.
//
// THE ADOPTED ARM IS FOR AN IMAGE THIS LANE BUILT AND FOUND AGAIN, NOT FOR A PUBLISHED ONE. A pull of
// somebody else's image is not a build with unestablished provenance; it is not a build at all, and
// an earlier revision minted one of these for it -- attaching a recipe and a build key to a pull, so
// the receipt printed a fallback key beside a published digest. That mint is deleted: the probe's
// outcome carries a reference and the digest the daemon reported, and nothing else.
//
// SO PROVENANCE IS ITS OWN FIELD AND IT HAS AN UNESTABLISHED ARM. A build this run performed and read
// back carries BuiltByThisRun and the recipe genuinely describes it. An image found already carrying
// the tag carries AdoptedUnderExpectedTag: the digest is real and the recipe is what WOULD have been
// built, and nothing here claims the two correspond. That is the same distinction
// extdeps.vllm.runtime_defaults draws with ImageSourceEquivalence, for the same reason -- an
// unestablished relation must not produce a positively stated fact.
// ── A THIRD PROVENANCE, BECAUSE A THIRD THING HAPPENED ────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE TWO EXISTING ARMS ARE "THIS RUN BUILT IT" AND "IT WAS ALREADY AT THE TAG WE COMPUTE". An image
// built OUT OF BAND is neither, and forcing it into the second would be the quieter lie of the two:
// `AdoptedUnderExpectedTag` says the tag was the one this model derives, which carries the build key,
// which carries the recipe. A hand-run build produced a hand-chosen tag, so even that much is unsaid.
//
// WHAT IS UNESTABLISHED IS BROADER THAN SOURCE EQUALITY. The adopted arm already disclaims that the
// source matches. For an out-of-band build the BUILD ARGUMENTS are equally unestablished: nothing
// recorded which architecture list, parallelism or target the invocation actually used, because the
// invocation was a shell line in a session rather than a spec this module rendered. Reading the
// checkout at the expected revision today says nothing about what yesterday's build compiled.
//
// IT IS MODELLED RATHER THAN DISCARDED BECAUSE THE BYTES ARE REAL AND EXPENSIVE, and because the
// alternative to naming this state is not having a cleaner state -- it is having the same image with
// no record of how it got here. The arm carries the observed tag so the artifact stays findable, and
// the reason so the limitation travels with it into every receipt that mentions it.
type VllmImageProvenance
  = BuiltByThisRun
  | AdoptedUnderExpectedTag
  | AdoptedFromOutOfBandBuild { observed_tag: NonEmptyStr, reason: NonEmptyStr }

fn vllm_image_provenance_wire(p: VllmImageProvenance) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match p {
    BuiltByThisRun => "built-by-this-run" as NonEmptyStr
    AdoptedUnderExpectedTag => "adopted-under-expected-tag (source equality UNESTABLISHED)" as NonEmptyStr
    AdoptedFromOutOfBandBuild { observed_tag: t, reason: why } =>
      join([
        "adopted-from-out-of-band-build tag=", t as String,
        " (source equality AND build-argument equality both UNESTABLISHED: ", why as String, ")",
      ], "") as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

// THE ONE ARTIFACT THIS ARM CURRENTLY DESCRIBES, recorded here rather than in a transcript. It is the
// GLM NoPE base image compiled on the build host on 2026-09-12 from vllm#55277's head, by an invocation
// whose arguments were derived from this module's rows and then typed into a shell by hand. What is
// known: the checkout it was built from reported the expected revision before the build, the guard line
// read `pe_dim must be 64 or 0`, and the model module was present in that tree. What is not known is
// everything a spec would have carried.
data glm53_nope_out_of_band_build_reason: NonEmptyStr = "built on 2026-09-12 by a hand-run docker invocation on the build host rather than through converge_vllm_runtime_image, at a point where the modelled route was wrongly believed to be unusable (see gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.an_access_deficit_diagnosed_wider_than_it_is). The arguments were transcribed from this module's recipe rows, but nothing recorded the invocation, so agreement between the image and any spec this module renders is unverified rather than merely unaudited" as NonEmptyStr

data glm53_nope_out_of_band_image_tag: NonEmptyStr = "gunbc-vllm-glm53-nope-gb10:8d09804-sm121" as NonEmptyStr

type VllmImageIdentity sole_constructor {
  tag: NonEmptyStr
  local_config_digest: NonEmptyStr
  requested_from: VllmSourceRevision
  build_key: ContentHash
  provenance: VllmImageProvenance
}

type VllmImageBuild
  = VllmImageBuilt { identity: VllmImageIdentity, already_present: Bool }
  | VllmImageBuildRefused { step: NonEmptyStr, cause: String }

func converge_vllm_image(ctx: SparkMaterializeContext, r: VllmContainerBuildRecipe) -> VllmImageBuild
  uses net: Network
{
  let tag = vllm_image_tag(r: r)
  match vllm_docker_leg(ctx: ctx, step: "image-digest" as NonEmptyStr, command: docker_image_digest_command(image: tag)) {
    ImageLegFailed { step: st, cause: why } =>
      VllmImageBuildRefused { step: st, cause: join(["the image query did not run, so nothing is known about whether the tag exists: ", why], "") }
    ImageLegRan { stdout: digest } =>
      if digest != "" {
        VllmImageBuilt {
          identity: VllmImageIdentity {
            tag: tag, local_config_digest: digest as NonEmptyStr, requested_from: r.source,
            build_key: vllm_image_build_key(r: r), provenance: AdoptedUnderExpectedTag,
          },
          already_present: true,
        }
      } else {
        match vllm_docker_leg(ctx: ctx, step: "image-build" as NonEmptyStr, command: docker_build_spec_command(spec: vllm_image_build_spec(r: r))) {
          ImageLegFailed { step: st, cause: why } => VllmImageBuildRefused { step: st, cause: why }
          ImageLegRan { stdout: _ } =>
            match vllm_docker_leg(ctx: ctx, step: "image-readback" as NonEmptyStr, command: docker_image_digest_command(image: tag)) {
              ImageLegFailed { step: st, cause: why } => VllmImageBuildRefused { step: st, cause: join(["the build reported success and the readback did not run: ", why], "") }
              ImageLegRan { stdout: readback } =>
                if readback == "" {
                  VllmImageBuildRefused { step: "image-readback" as NonEmptyStr, cause: "the build reported success and no image carries the tag it was asked to write" }
                } else {
                  VllmImageBuilt {
                    identity: VllmImageIdentity {
                      tag: tag, local_config_digest: readback as NonEmptyStr, requested_from: r.source,
                      build_key: vllm_image_build_key(r: r), provenance: BuiltByThisRun,
                    },
                    already_present: false,
                  }
                }
            }
        }
      }
  }
}

// ── THE ENTRY ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

data vllm_image_build_attempt_raw: String = "v41-runtime-image-build"
data vllm_image_build_receipt_path: String = "target/v41-runtime-image-build-receipt.txt"

fn vllm_image_receipt_line(b: VllmImageBuild) -> String {
  match b {
    VllmImageBuilt { identity: i, already_present: p } =>
      join([
        "image tag=", i.tag as String,
        " local_config_digest=", i.local_config_digest as String,
        " build_key=", serialize_content_hash(hash: i.build_key) as String,
        " requested_from=", i.requested_from.full_revision as String,
        " provenance=", vllm_image_provenance_wire(p: i.provenance) as String,
        " already_present=", if p { "true" } else { "false" }, "\n",
      ], "")
    VllmImageBuildRefused { step: st, cause: why } =>
      join(["image REFUSED step=", st as String, " cause=", why, "\n"], "")
  }
}

fn vllm_image_build_succeeded(b: VllmImageBuild) -> Bool {
  match b {
    VllmImageBuilt { identity: _, already_present: _ } => true
    VllmImageBuildRefused { step: _, cause: _ } => false
  }
}

func converge_vllm_runtime_image(target: SshTarget, context: FleetSshExecutionContext, ctx: SparkMaterializeContext, r: VllmContainerBuildRecipe) -> VllmImageBuild
  uses net: Network
{
  if !vllm_recipe_arch_is_buildable(r: r) {
    VllmImageBuildRefused {
      step: "arch-admission" as NonEmptyStr,
      cause: join([vllm_torch_cuda_arch_list_wire(archs: r.torch_cuda_arch_list), " is not within vLLM's CUDA_SUPPORTED_ARCHS at the pinned revision, so the build would reject it"], ""),
    }
  } else {
    match converge_vllm_source(target: target, context: context, r: r) {
      VllmSourceRefused { step: st, cause: why } => VllmImageBuildRefused { step: st, cause: why }
      VllmSourceAtRevision { revision: _, already_present: _ } => converge_vllm_image(ctx: ctx, r: r)
    }
  }
}

// THE BUILD IS ITS OWN ENTRY POINT, SEPARATE FROM THE PROBE, for the reason the canary's cutover is
// separate from its convergence: this one can occupy most of a host's spare capacity for hours, and
// an entry that did both would put that cost behind every attempt to re-read a capability.
// ── THE FALLBACK BUILD, AND WHAT IS UNRESOLVED ABOUT RUNNING IT ──────────────────────────────
//
// THE DOCKER LEGS NOW GO WHERE DOCKER CAN BE REACHED, which changes where this entry can run at all.
// An earlier revision took `--arg host=` and drove docker over the key session as the managed
// executor -- an account in no docker group on any Spark -- so it could only ever have worked on a
// host carrying provisioning drift, and that is precisely how it did work: srv5 has a hand-installed
// key for the human briansrls and that account in the docker group, which no modeled route produces.
// The fallback image exists because of an unaccounted-for state.
//
// 🟡 dissolve-on: vllm_runtime_image_build_ci_wet -- THE FALLBACK BUILD HAS NOT BEEN EXECUTED THROUGH
// THIS TRANSPORT. The docker legs are now the modeled privileged route, which means the entry needs
// the administrator credential and therefore the fleet-converge boundary, the same as the probe. What
// is NOT settled is whether a build fits there: gunbc_ci_spark_serving_step_timeout_minutes is 60 and
// the one executed build of this image took about 75 minutes cold, so a from-scratch run would be cut
// off mid-compile. DISSOLVES WHEN either a step timeout that admits a cold build is agreed (a job-level
// decision, not this lane's), or this entry is executed against a warm context and its receipt lands
// here. Until then the fallback build is correct by reading on this transport and executed only on the
// other one, and this row is what keeps that from being read the other way round.
// ── THE ENTRY TAKES A BUILD, AND THE ROOTS BELOW NAME WHICH ONE ───────────────────────────────
//
// THIS ENTRY HARDCODED `v41_gb10_recipe()` IN THREE PLACES -- the converge call, the receipt line and
// the source acquisition beside it -- so a second recipe could be declared, keyed, argument-rendered
// and never built. Replacing a hand-run build with "the modelled entry" would have rebuilt V4.1 while
// reporting the GLM request, which is the worst available outcome: a real receipt for the wrong bytes.
//
// IT IS PARAMETERISED RATHER THAN COPIED. A second GLM-shaped runner would duplicate the credential
// bracket, the trust materialisation, the host admission and the receipt -- five things that must not
// drift between two builds of the same fleet -- to vary one argument.
func vllm_runtime_image_build_for(b: FleetRuntimeBuild) -> ProcessExit
  uses net: Network
{
  let now = clock_now_probed_at()
  match spark_converge_target_selection(execute_apply: true) {
    SparkConvergeTargetRefused { reason: why } => exit_failure(reason: why)
    SparkConvergeTargetAllObserveOnly =>
      exit_failure(reason: "SPARK_CONVERGE_TARGET is unset: a build occupies one host for the better part of an hour, so it refuses rather than choosing one")
    SparkConvergeTargetSingle { host: build_host } =>
      if !spark_build_host_admissible(host: build_host) {
        exit_failure(reason: join([build_host as String, " is a rostered Spark but not an admissible BUILD host: ", join(map(admissible_spark_build_hosts(), h => h as String), ", "), " are the non-serving, non-reserved members"], ""))
      } else {
        match observe_executor_reach() {
          ExecutorReachUnknown { cause: c } => exit_failure(reason: join(["vllm_runtime_image_build_ci: ", c], ""))
          ExecutorReachKnown { short_hostname: executor, path: path } =>
            match read_bootstrap_credential() {
              BootstrapCredentialNotMaterialized { cause: c } => exit_failure(reason: c as String)
              BootstrapCredentialReady { secret: administrator } =>
                match fleet_ssh_attempt_identity(raw: vllm_image_build_attempt_raw) {
                  Absent => exit_failure(reason: "attempt identity is not a safe path segment")
                  Present { value: attempt } =>
                    match probe_agent_credential_verification(attempt: attempt) {
                      FleetSshCredentialIdentityMismatch { expected_line: expected, observed_line: observed } =>
                        exit_failure(reason: fleet_ssh_credential_verification_refusal_reason(verification: FleetSshCredentialIdentityMismatch { expected_line: expected, observed_line: observed }))
                      FleetSshCredentialDerivationRefused { cause: cause } =>
                        exit_failure(reason: fleet_ssh_credential_verification_refusal_reason(verification: FleetSshCredentialDerivationRefused { cause: cause }))
                      FleetSshCredentialVerified { binding: binding } => {
                        let materialization = materialize_fleet_known_hosts_anchor(attempt: attempt)
                        match fleet_ssh_execution_context_of(outcome: materialization, credential: binding) {
                          Absent => exit_failure(reason: join(["trust could not be established, so no leg ran: ", known_hosts_materialization_receipt(outcome: materialization)], ""))
                          Present { value: context } =>
                            match fleet_openssh_trust_policy_of(outcome: materialization) {
                              Absent => exit_failure(reason: "the trust policy could not be sealed for the privileged legs")
                              Present { value: trust } =>
                                match spark_reach_endpoint(host: build_host, path: ReachManagementLan) {
                                  Absent => exit_failure(reason: join(["no ", spark_reach_path_wire(p: ReachManagementLan), " endpoint for ", build_host as String], ""))
                                  Present { value: endpoint } =>
                                    match with_materialized_ssh_key_file(
                                      identity_file_name: "gunbc-vllm-image-build-admin-password" as NonEmptyStr,
                                      secret_bytes: administrator as Secret,
                                      use: fn(file) {
                                        converge_vllm_runtime_image(
                                          target: SshTarget { endpoint: endpoint as NonEmptyStr, principal: spark_managed_executor_login as NonEmptyStr },
                                          context: context,
                                          ctx: SparkMaterializeContext {
                                            host: build_host,
                                            trust: trust,
                                            path: path,
                                            admin_credential: administrator as Secret,
                                            admin_credential_file: file,
                                            executor: HostCli,
                                          },
                                          r: b.recipe,
                                        )
                                      },
                                    ) {
                                      MaterializedSshKeyFileBracketRefused { cause: c } => exit_failure(reason: c as String)
                                      MaterializedSshKeyFileBracketCompleted { value: build } => {
                                        let body = join([
                                          join(["vllm runtime image build probed_at=", probed_at_word(reading: now) as String, " host=", build_host as String, " endpoint=", endpoint, " executor=", executor, "\n"], ""),
                                          known_hosts_materialization_receipt(outcome: materialization),
                                          join([
                                            "recipe source=", b.recipe.source.full_revision as String,
                                            " arch_list=", vllm_torch_cuda_arch_list_wire(archs: b.recipe.torch_cuda_arch_list),
                                            " cuda=", b.recipe.cuda_version as String,
                                            " base=", b.recipe.build_base_image as String,
                                            " flashinfer=", flashinfer_acquisition_key(a: b.flashinfer),
                                            "\n"], ""),
                                          vllm_image_receipt_line(b: build),
                                        ], "")
                                        let w = Filesystem.Write(path: vllm_image_build_receipt_path, content: body)
                                        if !w.success {
                                          exit_failure(reason: join(["vllm runtime image build receipt write failed: ", w.error], ""))
                                        } else if vllm_image_build_succeeded(b: build) {
                                          ExitSuccess
                                        } else {
                                          exit_failure(reason: join(["vllm runtime image build did not converge:\n", body], ""))
                                        }
                                      }
                                    }
                                }
                            }
                        }
                      }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
      }
  }
}

// THE TWO BUILDS THIS FLEET HAS, EACH ITS OWN ENTRY OVER ONE BODY. Naming them separately is what
// keeps a caller from having to know which recipe is "the" recipe, and what makes the GLM build a
// thing an operator can ask for by name rather than by editing a hardcoded argument.
func vllm_runtime_image_build_ci_wet() -> ProcessExit
  uses net: Network
{
  vllm_runtime_image_build_for(b: v41_gb10_build())
}

// AND THE GLM BUILD, WHOSE SOURCE REVISION IS THE REPAIR AND WHOSE FLASHINFER IS A DERIVED-LAYER
// CONCERN. The base image this produces still carries upstream's pinned FlashInfer: the acquisition
// this build names cannot be reached by upstream's Dockerfile, so it lands in the derived layer and
// this entry's receipt records which acquisition the resulting image is INTENDED to be finished with.
func glm53_nope_image_build_wet() -> ProcessExit
  uses net: Network
{
  vllm_runtime_image_build_for(b: glm53_nope_build())
}

// ── MOVING AN IMAGE TO THE HOSTS THAT SERVE IT ────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE EXPORT AND THE IMPORT ARE DIFFERENT OPERATIONS ON DIFFERENT HOSTS, and the archive between them
// is a third artifact with its own identity. Writing this as one "ship" step would fuse three failures
// that owe three repairs: an export that did not produce a readable archive, a transfer that did not
// arrive, and a daemon that accepted an archive but now holds something other than what was asked for.
//
// THE LAST OF THOSE IS THE ONE THAT LOOKS LIKE SUCCESS. `docker load` reports that an archive was
// accepted; it does not say which image id sits behind the tag afterwards, and on a host that served a
// previous runtime the tag already exists. So the destination is READ BACK through the same privileged
// leg that loaded it, and the readback is compared to the identity the source host reported.
data image_archive_dir_name: NonEmptyStr = "image-archives" as NonEmptyStr

fn image_archive_dir() -> NonEmptyStr {
  join([vllm_build_root() as String, "/", image_archive_dir_name as String], "") as NonEmptyStr
}

// A TAG IS NOT A FILENAME, and rather than transliterate one into the other this derives the archive
// name from the CONTENT HASH the build already computes. Two references that differ only in a character
// a sanitiser would strip would otherwise share one archive, and the second export would overwrite the
// first silently; a content hash collides only when the inputs do.
fn image_archive_path(key: ContentHash) -> NonEmptyStr {
  join([image_archive_dir() as String, "/", serialize_content_hash(hash: key) as String, ".tar"], "") as NonEmptyStr
}

// ── EXPORT, ON THE HOST THAT HOLDS THE IMAGE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE ARCHIVE IS VERIFIED AS BYTES BEFORE IT TRAVELS, because a truncated export and a complete one are
// the same file to `scp`. The digest recorded here is the ARCHIVE's, and it is carried separately from
// the image identity for the whole journey: they answer different questions and a receipt that reported
// one as the other would be naming the envelope for the letter.
type ImageExportOutcome
  = ImageExported { archive_path: NonEmptyStr, archive_sha256: NonEmptyStr, source_image_id: NonEmptyStr }
  | ImageExportRefused { step: NonEmptyStr, cause: String }

func export_image_archive(ctx: SparkMaterializeContext, reference: NonEmptyStr, archive_key: ContentHash) -> ImageExportOutcome
  uses net: Network
{
  match read_image_identity(ctx: ctx, reference: reference) {
    ImageIdentityAbsent { reference: r } =>
      ImageExportRefused { step: "export-identity" as NonEmptyStr, cause: join([r as String, " is not present on the source host"], "") }
    ImageIdentityUnreadable { reference: _, cause: c } =>
      ImageExportRefused { step: "export-identity" as NonEmptyStr, cause: c }
    ImageIdentityObserved { reference: _, local_image_id: source_id } =>
      match vllm_docker_leg(ctx: ctx, step: "export-mkdir" as NonEmptyStr, command: mkdir_parents_command_at(mkdir_binary: mkdir_path_resolved_program, path: image_archive_dir() as String)) {
        ImageLegFailed { step: st, cause: why } => ImageExportRefused { step: st, cause: why }
        ImageLegRan { stdout: _ } =>
          match vllm_docker_leg(ctx: ctx, step: "export-save" as NonEmptyStr, command: docker_image_save_command(image: reference, destination: image_archive_path(key: archive_key))) {
            ImageLegFailed { step: st, cause: why } => ImageExportRefused { step: st, cause: why }
            ImageLegRan { stdout: _ } =>
              match vllm_docker_leg(ctx: ctx, step: "export-digest" as NonEmptyStr, command: sha256sum_digest_command(path: image_archive_path(key: archive_key) as String)) {
                ImageLegFailed { step: st, cause: why } => ImageExportRefused { step: st, cause: why }
                ImageLegRan { stdout: out } =>
                  match get(xs: split(s: trim(s: out), delimiter: " "), index: 0) {
                    Absent => ImageExportRefused { step: "export-digest" as NonEmptyStr, cause: "sha256sum produced no digest field" }
                    Present { value: d } =>
                      if d == "" {
                        ImageExportRefused { step: "export-digest" as NonEmptyStr, cause: "sha256sum produced an empty digest field" }
                      } else {
                        ImageExported { archive_path: image_archive_path(key: archive_key), archive_sha256: d as NonEmptyStr, source_image_id: source_id }
                      }
                  }
              }
          }
      }
  }
}

// ── IMPORT, ON EACH HOST THAT MUST SERVE IT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A HOST THAT ALREADY HOLDS THE RIGHT BYTES IS NOT RE-SENT. The check is the image id, not the tag:
// a tag that matches proves nothing, and this is the resume property -- an interrupted distribution
// should finish the hosts that are missing rather than repeat the ones that are done.
// THE HOST IN THE RESULT IS THE HOST THAT WAS READ, and it is taken from the context rather than supplied
// beside it. An earlier signature took both a context and a `host: HostIdentity` and labelled the outcome
// with the second while every read went through the first, so a caller could probe one machine and
// receive an outcome naming another -- and every consumer downstream, including the population join
// above, would treat that label as the fact. The context is the thing that executed; nothing else can
// honestly name where the answer came from.
func distribute_image_to(ctx: SparkMaterializeContext, reference: NonEmptyStr, expected_image_id: NonEmptyStr) -> ImageDistributionOutcome
  uses net: Network
{
  let host = ctx.host
  match read_image_identity(ctx: ctx, reference: reference) {
    ImageIdentityObserved { reference: _, local_image_id: observed } =>
      if (observed as String) == (expected_image_id as String) {
        ImagePresentOnHost { host: host, local_image_id: observed }
      } else {
        ImageIdentityMismatch { host: host, expected: expected_image_id, observed: observed }
      }
    ImageIdentityUnreadable { reference: _, cause: c } =>
      ImageTransferRefused { host: host, step: "destination-probe" as NonEmptyStr, cause: c }
    ImageIdentityAbsent { reference: _ } =>
      ImageTransferRefused {
        host: host,
        step: "destination-absent" as NonEmptyStr,
        cause: "the image is not on this host and the archive transfer leg is not yet bound; the population is incomplete rather than silently short",
      }
  }
}

// ── INSPECTING AN IMAGE THROUGH THE PRIVILEGED LEG, WHICH IS ALSO HOW THAT LEG GETS TESTED ────
//
// TWO QUESTIONS ARE ANSWERED BY ONE READ-ONLY RUN. The first is about ACCESS: whether this session can
// perform the build's privileged docker operations at all -- the bootstrap credential, the trust policy
// and `sudo -S` through the modelled performer, none of which the reachability probe touches, because
// that one authenticates as the managed principal and runs `hostname -s`. The second is about an
// ARTIFACT: what image id actually sits behind a tag, which is the evidence an adoption decision needs
// and cannot get from the tag itself.
//
// IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT GATED ON BUILD-HOST ADMISSIBILITY. `spark_build_host_admissible` answers where
// a build may RUN -- it excludes serving hosts because an hour of parallel compilation on a host holding
// a Ray rank is a real risk to a real service. Reading an image's identity costs one docker call and
// borrowing the build predicate here would refuse to look at images on exactly the hosts that serve
// them. The host is still admitted as a rostered Spark, which is the check that belongs to this
// operation.
//
// AND THE ABSENCE CONTRACT IS THE ONE `inspect` ACTUALLY HAS: it exits non-zero and says `No such
// image`, so a missing tag is a typed refusal rather than empty output. Confusing the two is how a host
// that does not have the image becomes a host that reported nothing.
type ImageIdentityRead
  = ImageIdentityObserved { reference: NonEmptyStr, local_image_id: NonEmptyStr }
  | ImageIdentityAbsent { reference: NonEmptyStr }
  | ImageIdentityUnreadable { reference: NonEmptyStr, cause: String }

func read_image_identity(ctx: SparkMaterializeContext, reference: NonEmptyStr) -> ImageIdentityRead
  uses net: Network
{
  match spark_remote_run(ctx: ctx, argv: argv_words(command: docker_image_inspect_command(image: reference))) {
    RemoteUnreachable { cause: why } => ImageIdentityUnreadable { reference: reference, cause: why }
    RemoteRan { exit_code: code, stdout: out, stderr: err } =>
      if code != 0 {
        if docker_image_inspect_names_no_such_image(stderr: err) {
          ImageIdentityAbsent { reference: reference }
        } else {
          ImageIdentityUnreadable { reference: reference, cause: join(["exit=", to_string(code), " stderr=", trim(s: err)], "") }
        }
      } else {
        match docker_image_inspect_id_from_stdout(stdout: out) {
          DockerImageInspectId { id: i } => ImageIdentityObserved { reference: reference, local_image_id: i }
          DockerImageInspectIdUnreadable { cause: c } => ImageIdentityUnreadable { reference: reference, cause: c }
        }
      }
  }
}

fn image_identity_read_line(r: ImageIdentityRead) -> String {
  match r {
    ImageIdentityObserved { reference: ref, local_image_id: i } =>
      join(["image reference=", ref as String, " local_image_id=", i as String, " privileged_leg=performed\n"], "")
    ImageIdentityAbsent { reference: ref } =>
      join(["image reference=", ref as String, " ABSENT on this host (inspect said No such image); privileged_leg=performed\n"], "")
    ImageIdentityUnreadable { reference: ref, cause: c } =>
      join(["image reference=", ref as String, " UNREAD: ", c, "\n"], "")
  }
}

// A READ THAT DID NOT HAPPEN IS NOT AN ABSENT IMAGE, and the access question reads the same split: an
// unreadable result is evidence about this session's privileged path, while an absent one is evidence
// that the path WORKED and the host does not hold that image.
fn privileged_leg_performed(r: ImageIdentityRead) -> Bool {
  match r {
    ImageIdentityObserved { reference: _, local_image_id: _ } => true
    ImageIdentityAbsent { reference: _ } => true
    ImageIdentityUnreadable { reference: _, cause: _ } => false
  }
}

// ── ACQUIRING THE SOURCE ON ITS OWN ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE FIRST PHASE OF THE BUILD, ENTERED SEPARATELY, because it is the phase that had never executed:
// every modeled run so far found the tree already present and returned without cloning anything, so
// the clone leg was correct by reading and not by execution -- and it was not correct, since the
// repository IDENTITY it was handed is not a locator git can resolve. Exercising it costs a shallow
// clone rather than the hours a full build costs, and it is the same `converge_vllm_source` the build
// entry calls: this is not a second path, it is the first one entered at its own door.
data vllm_source_acquire_attempt_raw: String = "vllm-source-acquire"
data vllm_source_acquire_receipt_path: String = "target/vllm-source-acquire-receipt.txt"

fn vllm_source_state_line(s: VllmSourceState) -> String {
  match s {
    VllmSourceAtRevision { revision: rev, already_present: p } =>
      join(["source revision=", rev as String, " already_present=", if p { "true" } else { "false" }, " clean=true\n"], "")
    VllmSourceRefused { step: st, cause: why } =>
      join(["source REFUSED step=", st as String, " cause=", why, "\n"], "")
  }
}

fn vllm_source_acquired(s: VllmSourceState) -> Bool {
  match s {
    VllmSourceAtRevision { revision: _, already_present: _ } => true
    VllmSourceRefused { step: _, cause: _ } => false
  }
}

func vllm_source_acquire_for(b: FleetRuntimeBuild, host: String) -> ProcessExit
  uses net: Network
{
  let now = clock_now_probed_at()
  match admit_spark_build_host(raw: host) {
    SparkBuildHostRefused { cause: why } => exit_failure(reason: why)
    SparkBuildHostAdmitted { host: build_host } =>
      match fleet_ssh_attempt_identity(raw: vllm_source_acquire_attempt_raw) {
        Absent => exit_failure(reason: "attempt identity is not a safe path segment")
        Present { value: attempt } =>
          match probe_agent_credential_verification(attempt: attempt) {
            FleetSshCredentialIdentityMismatch { expected_line: expected, observed_line: observed } =>
              exit_failure(reason: fleet_ssh_credential_verification_refusal_reason(verification: FleetSshCredentialIdentityMismatch { expected_line: expected, observed_line: observed }))
            FleetSshCredentialDerivationRefused { cause: cause } =>
              exit_failure(reason: fleet_ssh_credential_verification_refusal_reason(verification: FleetSshCredentialDerivationRefused { cause: cause }))
            FleetSshCredentialVerified { binding: binding } => {
              let materialization = materialize_fleet_known_hosts_anchor(attempt: attempt)
              match fleet_ssh_execution_context_of(outcome: materialization, credential: binding) {
                Absent => exit_failure(reason: join(["trust could not be established, so no leg ran: ", known_hosts_materialization_receipt(outcome: materialization)], ""))
                Present { value: context } =>
                  match spark_reach_endpoint(host: build_host, path: ReachManagementLan) {
                    Absent => exit_failure(reason: join(["no ", spark_reach_path_wire(p: ReachManagementLan), " endpoint for ", build_host as String], ""))
                    Present { value: endpoint } => {
                      let target = SshTarget { endpoint: endpoint as NonEmptyStr, principal: spark_managed_executor_login as NonEmptyStr }
                      let state = converge_vllm_source(target: target, context: context, r: b.recipe)
                      let body = join([
                        join(["vllm source acquire probed_at=", probed_at_word(reading: now) as String, " host=", build_host as String, " endpoint=", endpoint, " principal=", spark_managed_executor_login, "\n"], ""),
                        join(["root=", vllm_build_root() as String, " source=", vllm_source_dir_for(source: b.recipe.source) as String, " clone_url=", vllm_clone_url(source: b.recipe.source), "\n"], ""),
                        known_hosts_materialization_receipt(outcome: materialization),
                        vllm_source_state_line(s: state),
                      ], "")
                      let w = Filesystem.Write(path: vllm_source_acquire_receipt_path, content: body)
                      if !w.success {
                        exit_failure(reason: join(["vllm source acquire receipt write failed: ", w.error], ""))
                      } else if vllm_source_acquired(s: state) {
                        ExitSuccess
                      } else {
                        exit_failure(reason: join(["the source was not acquired at the pinned revision:\n", body], ""))
                      }
                    }
                  }
              }
            }
          }
      }
  }
}

// ── WHO INVOKES THE ENTRIES IN THIS MODULE (DESIGN §3c) ───────────────────────────────────────
//
// NONE OF THEM HAS AN IN-TREE CALLER, AND THAT IS THE SHAPE OF AN ACTUATOR RATHER THAN A GAP -- but
// only if the invocation is named, which is what this block does. An entry whose consumer is "someone
// will type it" is dangling; an entry whose consumer is a stated dispatch is not.
//
// THE ACQUISITION ROOTS, ONE PER BUILD, FOR THE SAME REASON THE BUILD ROOTS EXIST: a source tree is
// fetched for a REVISION, and which revision is the thing a caller is choosing when it asks for this.
func vllm_source_acquire_wet(host: String) -> ProcessExit
  uses net: Network
{
  vllm_source_acquire_for(b: v41_gb10_build(), host: host)
}

func glm53_nope_source_acquire_wet(host: String) -> ProcessExit
  uses net: Network
{
  vllm_source_acquire_for(b: glm53_nope_build(), host: host)
}

// vllm_source_acquire_wet and spark_build_host_reachability_wet are OPERATOR-INVOKED through the
// ordinary CLI -- `gunbc run --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --entry
// dag/gunbc/spark/vllm_runtime_image_build.dag --function <name> [--arg host=<identity>]` -- which is
// how every read-only fleet instrument here is reached, and both have been EXECUTED that way against
// srv9, with their receipts in this lane's record. They take the fleet key from an in-run agent and
// need no credential an authoring session cannot hold, which is why they are CLI entries rather than
// dispatch modes.
//
// vllm_runtime_image_build_ci_wet is DIFFERENT and is a declared frontier: it needs the administrator
// credential, so its only honest invocation is a fleet-converge mode beside spark_runtime_image_probe.
// That mode is not added here, for a job-level reason stated in the dissolve-on beside the entry.
// Until it exists the entry is reachable only by an operator who materializes the credential
// themselves, and this paragraph is the record that its dispatch is owed.

// ── CAN THIS LANE REACH THE HOST IT WAS GIVEN ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A PRECONDITION OF BOTH ENTRIES, ASKED ON ITS OWN. Admissibility says a host is one this lane MAY
// use; it says nothing about whether the fleet key is installed on it or whether anything answers at
// its endpoint. Those are different failures with different remedies -- a policy answer, a
// provisioning answer -- and discovering the second in the middle of a build is how an hour gets
// spent before the first leg refuses.
//
// IT ASKS ONE QUESTION AND NOTHING ELSE: the remote host's own short hostname, over the same sealed
// transport every other leg uses. What comes back is the host's answer about its own identity, which
// is exactly the join a caller needs -- an endpoint resolved from a fleet identity should answer with
// that identity's machine, and a reachable box that answers with a different name is a fleet-data
// defect rather than a reachable host.
// THE JOIN IS PERFORMED, NOT DESCRIBED. An earlier revision captured `reported_hostname` and compared
// it to nothing, while the annotation above claimed the comparison as the point of the probe -- a
// promise in prose that the code did not keep, which is worse than not making it.
//
// AND THE NAME IT IS COMPARED TO IS NOT THE IDENTITY. A Spark answers `hostname -s` with its VENDOR
// LABEL (spark-a3ee), never with its fleet identity (srv5); asserting equality against the identity
// would call every host in the fleet a defect. The label bound to an identity is recorded by
// gunbc.spark.fabric_switch_observed as `spark_hostname` on that host's lane observation, so the join
// is between two independently obtained facts: a lane reading taken earlier, and the box's own answer
// now. A host with no lane row cannot be joined at all, which is its own arm rather than a pass.
type SparkHostReachability
  = SparkHostAnswered { host: HostIdentity, endpoint: NonEmptyStr, reported_hostname: NonEmptyStr }
  | SparkHostIdentityMismatch { host: HostIdentity, endpoint: NonEmptyStr, expected: NonEmptyStr, reported: NonEmptyStr }
  | SparkHostIdentityUnjoinable { host: HostIdentity, endpoint: NonEmptyStr, reported: NonEmptyStr }
  | SparkHostUnreachable { host: HostIdentity, endpoint: NonEmptyStr, cause: String }
  | SparkHostUnresolved { host: HostIdentity }

fn spark_expected_reported_hostname(host: HostIdentity) -> NonEmptyStr? {
  match fabric_lane_for_host(host: host) {
    Absent => none
    Present { value: o } => Present { value: o.spark_hostname }
  }
}

fn classify_reported_hostname(host: HostIdentity, endpoint: NonEmptyStr, reported: NonEmptyStr) -> SparkHostReachability {
  match spark_expected_reported_hostname(host: host) {
    Absent => SparkHostIdentityUnjoinable { host: host, endpoint: endpoint, reported: reported }
    Present { value: expected } =>
      if (expected as String) == (reported as String) {
        SparkHostAnswered { host: host, endpoint: endpoint, reported_hostname: reported }
      } else {
        SparkHostIdentityMismatch { host: host, endpoint: endpoint, expected: expected, reported: reported }
      }
  }
}

func probe_spark_host_reachability(context: FleetSshExecutionContext, host: HostIdentity) -> SparkHostReachability
  uses net: Network
{
  match spark_reach_endpoint(host: host, path: ReachManagementLan) {
    Absent => SparkHostUnresolved { host: host }
    Present { value: endpoint } => {
      let target = SshTarget { endpoint: endpoint as NonEmptyStr, principal: spark_managed_executor_login as NonEmptyStr }
      match vllm_image_leg(target: target, context: context, step: "reachability" as NonEmptyStr, command: hostname_short_read_command()) {
        ImageLegFailed { step: _, cause: why } => SparkHostUnreachable { host: host, endpoint: endpoint as NonEmptyStr, cause: why }
        ImageLegRan { stdout: name } =>
          if name == "" {
            SparkHostUnreachable { host: host, endpoint: endpoint as NonEmptyStr, cause: "the leg succeeded and the host reported no name" }
          } else {
            classify_reported_hostname(host: host, endpoint: endpoint as NonEmptyStr, reported: name as NonEmptyStr)
          }
      }
    }
  }
}

fn spark_host_answered(r: SparkHostReachability) -> Bool {
  match r {
    SparkHostAnswered { host: _, endpoint: _, reported_hostname: _ } => true
    SparkHostIdentityMismatch { host: _, endpoint: _, expected: _, reported: _ } => false
    SparkHostIdentityUnjoinable { host: _, endpoint: _, reported: _ } => false
    SparkHostUnreachable { host: _, endpoint: _, cause: _ } => false
    SparkHostUnresolved { host: _ } => false
  }
}

fn spark_reachability_line(r: SparkHostReachability) -> String {
  match r {
    SparkHostAnswered { host: h, endpoint: e, reported_hostname: name } =>
      join(["reachable host=", h as String, " endpoint=", e as String, " reported=", name as String, " fleet_key=admitted\n"], "")
    SparkHostIdentityMismatch { host: h, endpoint: e, expected: want, reported: got } =>
      join(["IDENTITY MISMATCH host=", h as String, " endpoint=", e as String, " expected=", want as String, " reported=", got as String, " -- the endpoint answered, as a different machine than the fleet model binds to this identity\n"], "")
    SparkHostIdentityUnjoinable { host: h, endpoint: e, reported: got } =>
      join(["unjoinable host=", h as String, " endpoint=", e as String, " reported=", got as String, " -- no lane observation records a hostname for this identity, so the answer cannot be checked against anything\n"], "")
    SparkHostUnreachable { host: h, endpoint: e, cause: why } =>
      join(["unreachable host=", h as String, " endpoint=", e as String, " cause=", why, "\n"], "")
    SparkHostUnresolved { host: h } =>
      join(["unresolved host=", h as String, " -- no management-LAN endpoint\n"], "")
  }
}

data spark_reachability_attempt_raw: String = "spark-build-host-reachability"
data spark_reachability_receipt_path: String = "target/spark-build-host-reachability-receipt.txt"

// THE WHOLE ADMISSIBLE ROSTER IN ONE RUN, because the question a caller actually has is "which of
// these may I be given", not "is this one alive".
// A RECEIPT THAT WROTE IS NOT A LINE THAT RAN. An earlier revision of this entry returned success
// whenever the receipt file landed, so the run in which every admissible host refused the fleet key
// -- which is what actually happened -- exited zero, and any caller gating on it would have proceeded.
// The diagnostics were typed and located and then discarded by the exit path, which is §5's failure
// arm widening into silence. It now succeeds only when every admissible host answered AND its answer
// joined to the identity it was asked about.
// ── THE PRIVILEGED-PATH CHECK, ASKED OF ONE HOST AND ONE IMAGE ────────────────────────────────
//
// THIS IS THE PREREQUISITE THE REACHABILITY SCAN DOES NOT ANSWER. That one verifies the fleet key
// against the agent, materialises the trust anchor, connects as the MANAGED principal and reads a
// hostname. Everything the build does with docker goes down a different path -- the bootstrap
// credential, the administrator secret materialised to a file, `sudo -S` through the privileged
// performer -- and none of it is exercised by a hostname read. A green reachability receipt beside a
// broken administrator path is exactly the state that sends someone back to hand-written ssh.
//
// IT IS ONE HOST AND NOT A SCAN, for the same reason. An aggregate over every admissible host can fail
// because an unrelated machine is down, or pass over an empty population, and neither answers the
// question a build is about to ask: can THIS executor perform THIS operation.
data spark_image_inspect_attempt_raw: String = "spark-image-identity-inspect"
data spark_image_inspect_receipt_path: String = "target/spark-image-identity-receipt.txt"

// READING AN IMAGE IS NOT BUILDING ONE, and this entry takes the host as given rather than through the
// build admission. `admit_spark_build_host` folds two questions into one refusal -- is this a rostered
// Spark, and may a BUILD run on it -- and only the first belongs here: the images this fleet serves live
// on serving hosts by construction, so borrowing the build predicate would refuse to look at exactly the
// artifacts worth looking at. The host identity is still the modelled one.
func spark_image_identity_wet(host_raw: String, reference_raw: String) -> ProcessExit
  uses net: Network
{
  let now = clock_now_probed_at()
  if host_raw == "" {
    exit_failure(reason: "a host is required")
  } else {
    spark_image_identity_on(host: host_raw as HostIdentity, reference_raw: reference_raw, now: now)
  }
}

func spark_image_identity_on(host: HostIdentity, reference_raw: String, now: NonEmptyStr?) -> ProcessExit
  uses net: Network
{
  if reference_raw == "" {
    exit_failure(reason: "an image reference is required")
  } else {
    let reference = reference_raw as NonEmptyStr
      match fleet_ssh_attempt_identity(raw: spark_image_inspect_attempt_raw) {
        Absent => exit_failure(reason: "attempt identity is not a safe path segment")
        Present { value: attempt } =>
          match probe_agent_credential_verification(attempt: attempt) {
            FleetSshCredentialIdentityMismatch { expected_line: expected, observed_line: observed } =>
              exit_failure(reason: fleet_ssh_credential_verification_refusal_reason(verification: FleetSshCredentialIdentityMismatch { expected_line: expected, observed_line: observed }))
            FleetSshCredentialDerivationRefused { cause: cause } =>
              exit_failure(reason: fleet_ssh_credential_verification_refusal_reason(verification: FleetSshCredentialDerivationRefused { cause: cause }))
            FleetSshCredentialVerified { binding: binding } => {
              let materialization = materialize_fleet_known_hosts_anchor(attempt: attempt)
              match fleet_ssh_execution_context_of(outcome: materialization, credential: binding) {
                Absent => exit_failure(reason: join(["trust could not be established, so no leg ran: ", known_hosts_materialization_receipt(outcome: materialization)], ""))
                Present { value: context } =>
                  match fleet_openssh_trust_policy_of(outcome: materialization) {
                    Absent => exit_failure(reason: "the trust policy could not be sealed for the privileged leg")
                    Present { value: trust } =>
                      match observe_executor_reach() {
                        ExecutorReachUnknown { cause: c } => exit_failure(reason: join(["executor reach: ", c], ""))
                        ExecutorReachKnown { short_hostname: _, path: path } =>
                          match read_bootstrap_credential() {
                            BootstrapCredentialNotMaterialized { cause: c } => exit_failure(reason: c as String)
                            BootstrapCredentialReady { secret: administrator } =>
                              match with_materialized_ssh_key_file(
                                identity_file_name: "gunbc-image-identity-admin-password" as NonEmptyStr,
                                secret_bytes: administrator as Secret,
                                use: fn(file) {
                                  read_image_identity(
                                    ctx: SparkMaterializeContext {
                                      host: host,
                                      trust: trust,
                                      path: path,
                                      admin_credential: administrator as Secret,
                                      admin_credential_file: file,
                                      executor: HostCli,
                                    },
                                    reference: reference,
                                  )
                                },
                              ) {
                                MaterializedSshKeyFileBracketRefused { cause: c } => exit_failure(reason: c as String)
                                MaterializedSshKeyFileBracketCompleted { value: read } => {
                                  let body = join([
                                    join(["image identity probed_at=", probed_at_word(reading: now) as String, " host=", host as String, "\n"], ""),
                                    known_hosts_materialization_receipt(outcome: materialization),
                                    image_identity_read_line(r: read),
                                  ], "")
                                  let w = Filesystem.Write(path: spark_image_inspect_receipt_path, content: body)
                                  if !w.success {
                                    exit_failure(reason: "the identity receipt could not be written")
                                  } else {
                                    if privileged_leg_performed(r: read) { ExitSuccess }
                                    else { exit_failure(reason: image_identity_read_line(r: read)) }
                                  }
                                }
                              }
                          }
                      }
                  }
              }
            }
          }
      }
  }
}

func spark_build_host_reachability_wet() -> ProcessExit
  uses net: Network
{
  let now = clock_now_probed_at()
  match fleet_ssh_attempt_identity(raw: spark_reachability_attempt_raw) {
    Absent => exit_failure(reason: "attempt identity is not a safe path segment")
    Present { value: attempt } =>
      match probe_agent_credential_verification(attempt: attempt) {
        FleetSshCredentialIdentityMismatch { expected_line: expected, observed_line: observed } =>
          exit_failure(reason: fleet_ssh_credential_verification_refusal_reason(verification: FleetSshCredentialIdentityMismatch { expected_line: expected, observed_line: observed }))
        FleetSshCredentialDerivationRefused { cause: cause } =>
          exit_failure(reason: fleet_ssh_credential_verification_refusal_reason(verification: FleetSshCredentialDerivationRefused { cause: cause }))
        FleetSshCredentialVerified { binding: binding } => {
          let materialization = materialize_fleet_known_hosts_anchor(attempt: attempt)
          match fleet_ssh_execution_context_of(outcome: materialization, credential: binding) {
            Absent => exit_failure(reason: join(["trust could not be established, so no leg ran: ", known_hosts_materialization_receipt(outcome: materialization)], ""))
            Present { value: context } => {
              let readings = map(admissible_spark_build_hosts(), h => probe_spark_host_reachability(context: context, host: h))
              let body = join([
                join(["spark build-host reachability probed_at=", probed_at_word(reading: now) as String, " question=short hostname only\n"], ""),
                join(["credential ", fleet_ssh_credential_locus_label(binding: binding), " public_material_identity=", serialize_content_hash(hash: fleet_ssh_credential_identity(binding: binding)) as String, "\n"], ""),
                join(map(readings, r => spark_reachability_line(r: r)), ""),
              ], "")
              let w = Filesystem.Write(path: spark_reachability_receipt_path, content: body)
              if !w.success {
                exit_failure(reason: join(["spark reachability receipt write failed: ", w.error], ""))
              } else if fold(readings, init: true, f: (acc, r) => acc && spark_host_answered(r: r)) {
                ExitSuccess
              } else {
                exit_failure(reason: join(["not every admissible build host answered:\n", body], ""))
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
  }
}

// ── ACQUIRING THE WHEELS ONTO THE BUILD HOST, AS AN ENTRY THAT ACTUALLY RUNS ──────────────────
//
// THE FOLD AND ITS REMOTE BINDING BOTH EXISTED AND NOTHING CALLED EITHER. `materialize_wheel_population`
// compiled, its handlers compiled, its fixtures were green, and no entry point anywhere in this corpus
// reached them -- the dangling declaration DESIGN section 3c names, in the form that is hardest to see,
// because every piece is individually correct and only the absence of a caller is wrong.
//
// THE PARENT DIRECTORY IS THE RETENTION DIRECTORY AND NOT A SCRATCH PATH, because the whole point of
// keeping the wheels is that a nightly index withdraws builds: bytes that land under the build root
// outlive the build and let a rebuild proceed when the channel has moved on.
//
// AND THE HOST IS ADMITTED AS A BUILD HOST RATHER THAN TAKEN AS GIVEN. These bytes exist to be installed
// by a build, so the machine they land on is the machine that will build -- putting them anywhere else
// produces a verified path that the build cannot open.
data spark_wheel_fetch_attempt_raw: String = "spark-flashinfer-wheel-fetch"
data spark_wheel_fetch_receipt_path: String = "target/spark-flashinfer-wheel-receipt.txt"

func spark_fetch_wheels_wet(host_raw: String) -> ProcessExit
  uses net: Network
{
  let now = clock_now_probed_at()
  match admit_spark_build_host(raw: host_raw) {
    SparkBuildHostRefused { cause: c } => exit_failure(reason: c)
    SparkBuildHostAdmitted { host: host } => spark_fetch_wheels_on(host: host, now: now)
  }
}

func spark_fetch_wheels_on(host: HostIdentity, now: NonEmptyStr?) -> ProcessExit
  uses net: Network
{
  match fleet_ssh_attempt_identity(raw: spark_wheel_fetch_attempt_raw) {
    Absent => exit_failure(reason: "attempt identity is not a safe path segment")
    Present { value: attempt } =>
      match probe_agent_credential_verification(attempt: attempt) {
        FleetSshCredentialIdentityMismatch { expected_line: expected, observed_line: observed } =>
          exit_failure(reason: fleet_ssh_credential_verification_refusal_reason(verification: FleetSshCredentialIdentityMismatch { expected_line: expected, observed_line: observed }))
        FleetSshCredentialDerivationRefused { cause: cause } =>
          exit_failure(reason: fleet_ssh_credential_verification_refusal_reason(verification: FleetSshCredentialDerivationRefused { cause: cause }))
        FleetSshCredentialVerified { binding: binding } => {
          let materialization = materialize_fleet_known_hosts_anchor(attempt: attempt)
          match fleet_ssh_execution_context_of(outcome: materialization, credential: binding) {
            Absent => exit_failure(reason: join(["trust could not be established, so no leg ran: ", known_hosts_materialization_receipt(outcome: materialization)], ""))
            Present { value: context } =>
              match fleet_openssh_trust_policy_of(outcome: materialization) {
                Absent => exit_failure(reason: "the trust policy could not be sealed for the privileged leg")
                Present { value: trust } =>
                  match observe_executor_reach() {
                    ExecutorReachUnknown { cause: c } => exit_failure(reason: join(["executor reach: ", c], ""))
                    ExecutorReachKnown { short_hostname: _, path: path } =>
                      match read_bootstrap_credential() {
                        BootstrapCredentialNotMaterialized { cause: c } => exit_failure(reason: c as String)
                        BootstrapCredentialReady { secret: administrator } =>
                          match with_materialized_ssh_key_file(
                            identity_file_name: "gunbc-wheel-fetch-admin-password" as NonEmptyStr,
                            secret_bytes: administrator as Secret,
                            use: fn(file) {
                              materialize_wheels_on(
                                ctx: SparkMaterializeContext {
                                  host: host,
                                  trust: trust,
                                  path: path,
                                  admin_credential: administrator as Secret,
                                  admin_credential_file: file,
                                  executor: HostCli,
                                },
                                ws: flashinfer_0_7_0_dev20260910_wheels,
                                parent: wheel_retention_directory(),
                              )
                            },
                          ) {
                            MaterializedSshKeyFileBracketRefused { cause: c } => exit_failure(reason: c as String)
                            MaterializedSshKeyFileBracketCompleted { value: at } => {
                              let body = join([
                                join(["flashinfer wheels probed_at=", probed_at_word(reading: now) as String, " host=", at.host as String, "\n"], ""),
                                known_hosts_materialization_receipt(outcome: materialization),
                                wheel_population_receipt(at: at),
                              ], "")
                              let w = Filesystem.Write(path: spark_wheel_fetch_receipt_path, content: body)
                              if !w.success {
                                exit_failure(reason: "the wheel acquisition receipt could not be written")
                              } else {
                                match at.population {
                                  WheelsReady { paths: _ } => ExitSuccess
                                  WheelsIncomplete { failures: _, ready: _ } => exit_failure(reason: wheel_population_receipt(at: at))
                                }
                              }
                            }
                          }
                      }
                  }
              }
          }
        }
      }
  }
}

// THE RECEIPT NAMES THE HOST ON EVERY LINE, because a wheel path is only a fact beside the machine it is
// on, and a receipt read later by a person or by a build context assembler must not have to remember
// which host the run was about.
fn wheel_population_receipt(at: WheelPopulationAt) -> String {
  match at.population {
    WheelsReady { wheels: ws } => join(map(ws, w => acquired_wheel_line(at: at, w: w)), "")
    WheelsIncomplete { failures: fs, ready: ws } =>
      join([
        join(map(ws, w => acquired_wheel_line(at: at, w: w)), ""),
        join(map(fs, f => join(["wheel MISSING host=", at.host as String, " ", wheel_materialization_line(m: f)], "")), ""),
      ], "")
  }
}

fn acquired_wheel_line(at: WheelPopulationAt, w: AcquiredWheel) -> String {
  join(["wheel ready host=", at.host as String, " name=", w.filename as String, " path=", w.install_path as String, "\n"], "")
}
